t if they're in the same place and same time, their fossils are from the same period. But option B is saying that different species in the same place belong to different periods, which is the opposite. So if that were true, their method wouldn't work because they rely on multiple species being from the same period. So B is actually the opposite of what they need, so B is not the assumption they depend on.\n\nOption C: The method of determining the date of beetles is more accurate than that of other organisms. The context says they selected existing biological fossils and determined their dates. It doesn't compare the accuracy of dating beetles versus other organisms. They just used the dates determined for the beetles. So whether their dating method is more accurate isn't an assumption they're making; they're just using whatever dating method they have. So C doesn't seem to be the right assumption.\n\nOption D: In the past 22,000 years, the tolerable temperature of beetles has not changed significantly. This makes sense because if beetles' tolerable temperatures had changed a lot, then using current beetles' tolerances to infer past temperatures would be unreliable. The researchers are assuming that the beetles' temperature tolerance hasn't changed much over time, so they can apply current data to past conditions. That seems like a necessary assumption for their method to be valid.\n\nSo putting it all together, the correct answer should be D.\n</think>\n\nThe researchers' method relies on the assumption that beetles' tolerable temperatures have remained relatively stable over the past 22,000 years. This allows them to use current beetles' temperature data to infer past temperatures accurately.\n\n**Answer: D**",
