ight not be the assumption.\n\nOption C: There are no birdlike dinosaur fossils that are older than the bird fossils but have not yet been unearthed. Oh, this is interesting. The argument assumes that all the birdlike dinosaur fossils we've found are the earliest, and there are no older ones waiting to be discovered. Because if there were older birdlike dinosaurs, then the argument would be invalid. So the argument relies on the assumption that we've found all the relevant fossils, or at least that there are no older ones. So C seems like a possible assumption.\n\nOption D: It could not have been the case that some birds were descendants from one of the birdlike dinosaur species and other birds from another. This is talking about multiple origins. The argument doesn't address whether birds have multiple ancestors or not. It just says that because bird fossils are older, they can't be descended from birdlike dinosaurs. So D doesn't seem to be the assumption.\n\nSo putting it together, the argument assumes that there are no older birdlike dinosaur fossils that we haven't found yet. If such fossils existed, the argument would be wrong. Therefore, the assumption is C.\n</think>\n\nThe argument assumes that all relevant birdlike dinosaur fossils have been found, meaning there are no older ones undiscovered. This is necessary for the conclusion that birds can't be descended from them. \n\n**Answer: C**",
