 that in a similar situation, people don't make the same conclusion about the program's existence, just as they shouldn't about consciousness.\n</think>\n\nThe example of the damaged radio is used to draw an analogy where, despite the radio ceasing to function, we don't conclude the program has ended. This is compared to the situation where the brain is damaged, and consciousness is lost, but we shouldn't conclude consciousness has ended. Thus, the radio example serves as an analogous case to illustrate that people don't draw the same conclusion about the program's existence as they might about consciousness.\n\n**Answer: D**",
