      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I've got this logical reasoning problem here, and I need to figure out the answer. Let me try to break it down step by step. \n\nFirst, the context is about a committee that works for two years. Each year, the committee has 4 members: 2 judges and 2 scientists. The judges are F, G, H, and I, and the scientists are V, Y, and Z. Each year, one member chairs the committee. There are some specific rules to follow:\n\n1. The chairman of the first year must retire in the second year. So, whoever chairs the first year can't be on the committee at all in the second year.\n\n2. The chairman of the second year must have been a member of the committee in the first year. So, the second chairman has to have been part of the first year's committee but isn't necessarily the same as the first chairman.\n\n3. There are some constraints:\n   - G and V can't be on the committee in the same year.\n   - H and Y can't be on the committee in the same year.\n   - Each year, only one of I or V can be a member. So, if I is on the committee, V can't be, and vice versa.\n\nThe question is: If H is the chairman in the first year, who can be the chairman in the second year? The options are A) F, B) G, C) Y, D) I.\n\nAlright, let's start by mapping out the first year's committee. Since H is the chairman, he must be one of the two judges. So, the first year's judges are H and one other from F, G, or I. The scientists must be two from V, Y, Z, but with some constraints.\n\nWait, but each year, only one of I or V can be a member. So, in the first year, if I is a judge, then V can't be a scientist. Alternatively, if V is a scientist, then I can't be a judge.\n\nAlso, H and Y can't be on the committee in the same year. Since H is already a judge in the first year, Y can't be a scientist in the first year. So, the scientists in the first year must be two from V and Z, but considering the I/V constraint.\n\nLet me outline the first year's committee:\n\nChairman: H (judge