nNow, moving to the second year. The chairman of the first year (H) must retire, so H can't be on the committee in the second year. The chairman of the second year must have been a member of the first year's committee. So, the possible chairmen for the second year are F, V, or Z.\n\nWait, but the options given are F, G, Y, I. So, let's see which of these could be the chairman in the second year.\n\nWait, but in the first year, the committee members are H, F, V, Z. So, the second year's chairman must be one of these four, but H is retired, so it must be F, V, or Z.\n\nBut the options are A) F, B) G, C) Y, D) I. So, F is an option (A), but V and Z are not among the options. So, perhaps F is a possible chairman.\n\nBut let's check the constraints for the second year.\n\nIn the second year, the committee must again have 2 judges and 2 scientists, with the same constraints.\n\nAlso, the chairman of the second year must have been a member of the first year's committee, so it's F, V, or Z.\n\nBut the options are F, G, Y, I. So, only F is among the options. But let's make sure.\n\nWait, perhaps I made a mistake in the first year's committee. Let me try another approach.\n\nFirst year's committee:\n\nChairman: H (judge)\n\nJudges: H and one more (either F, G, or I). But as we saw earlier, if I is a judge, then V can't be a scientist, but we need two scientists, which would require V and Z, but V can't be there if I is a judge. So, I can't be a judge in the first year. Therefore, the other judge must be F or G.\n\nSo, let's say the other judge is F. Then, the scientists must be two from V, Y, Z, but Y can't be there because H is present. So, scientists are V and Z.\n\nSo, first year's committee: H, F, V, Z.\n\nNow, second year's committee:\n\nChairman must be one of F, V, Z.\n\nBut the options are F, G, Y, I. So, F is possible.\n\nBut let's check the constraints for the second year.\n\nIn the second year, the committee can't have H, as he's retired. So, the judges must be two