contradicts our earlier conclusion that W is off. Therefore, Y cannot be on. So, Y must be off. So, Y = Off.\n\nPutting it all together, the current state is:\n\n- S = On\n- T = On\n- W = Off\n- X = Off\n- Y = Off\n- Z = On\n\nNow, let's look at the options:\n\nA) T is turned on and Y is turned off.\n- T is on (yes), Y is off (yes). So A is true.\n\nB) Both T and Y are turned off.\n- T is on, so B is false.\n\nC) Both W and T are turned on.\n- W is off, so C is false.\n\nD) X is turned on and Y is turned off.\n- X is off, so D is false.\n\nSo, the only correct option is A.\n\nWait, but let me double-check. Did I miss anything? Let me go through each condition again with the current state.\n\nCondition 1: W is off, so X can be off. That's fine.\n\nCondition 2: S is on, so T must be on. Correct.\n\nCondition 3: T is on, X is off. They are not both on or both off. Correct.\n\nCondition 4: Y is off, so even though Z is on, Y is off, so the condition doesn't require W to be on. Correct.\n\nYes, everything checks out. So, the answer should be A.\n</think>\n\nThe correct answer is A) T is turned on and Y is turned off.\n\n**Step-by-Step Explanation:**\n\n1. **Given:** S and Z are on.\n2. **Condition 2:** Since S is on, T must be on.\n3. **Condition 3:** T is on, so X must be off.\n4. **Condition 1:** X is off, so W must be off.\n5. **Condition 4:** Z is on, but W is off, so Y must be off to avoid contradiction.\n\nThus, T is on and Y is off.\n\n**Answer:** A) T is turned on and Y is turned off.",
