      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I need to solve this logical reasoning problem. Let me read through the context and the question carefully.\n\nThe context is a conversation between a department manager and a general manager. The department manager wants to replace M-type computers with V-type ones. The reason given is that training new employees to use V-type computers would reduce costs by 20%. The general manager opposes this, saying that hiring employees who already know how to use M-type computers is a better solution.\n\nThe question is asking which option, if true, would most seriously weaken the general manager's opposition. So, I need to find an option that undermines the GM's argument against replacing M with V.\n\nLet me break down the GM's argument. The GM is saying that instead of switching to V-type computers, they can just hire people who already know how to use M-type. This avoids the need to train new employees on V-type, which the department manager is suggesting as a cost-saving measure.\n\nSo, the GM's position relies on the assumption that it's feasible and beneficial to hire people who already know M-type. If that assumption is weakened, the GM's opposition would be weakened.\n\nLooking at the options:\n\nOption A: All employees are required to learn M-type with new functions. Hmm, this might not directly affect the GM's argument. If everyone is learning M-type, maybe it's still easy to hire people who know M. But I'm not sure if this weakens the GM's position.\n\nOption B: Once employees learn computers, they're more likely to change jobs. Wait, if employees are more likely to leave after learning, then relying on hiring people who already know M might not be sustainable. Because those employees might leave, and then the company would have to hire new people who don't know M, which would require training. So this could weaken the GM's argument because it makes the strategy of hiring M-skilled employees less reliable.\n\nOption C: Employees proficient in M-type demand higher income. This suggests that hiring M-skilled employees is more expensive. If that's the case, the GM's alternative of hiring M-skilled employees becomes more costly, which would make the department manager's proposal of switching to V-type (which might have lower training costs) more attractive. So this could weaken the GM's opposition.\n\nOption D: The company's average productivity is lower than competitors. This seems unrelated to the argument about training costs and hiring. It's more about productivity, which isn't directly tied to the issue of computer types.\n\nNow, I need to decide between options B and C. Which one weakens the GM's position more.\n\nThe GM's argument is that hiring M-skilled employees is a better alternative to switching to V. So, if hiring M-skilled employees is more expensive (option C), that weakens the GM's argument because it's not as cost-effective as the GM thought. Alternatively, if employees are likely to leave after learning (option B), then the GM's strategy might not be sustainable because the company would have to keep hiring new people who know M, which might not be feasible.\n\nBut wait, the GM's argument is that they can hire people who already know M, so the cost of training is avoided. If option C is true, then hiring M-skilled employees is more expensive, which makes the GM's alternative less attractive. So the GM's reasoning that it's better to hire M-skilled employees is weakened because it's more costly.\n\nOption B, on the other hand, talks about employees changing jobs more after learning. This might affect the company's ability to retain employees, but it doesn't directly address the cost of hiring M-skilled employees. It's more about turnover, which is a different issue.\n\nSo, I think option C is the better choice because it directly impacts the cost-effectiveness of the GM's alternative. If M-skilled employees demand higher pay, then the GM's argument that it's better to hire them (to avoid training costs) is weakened because the hiring cost is higher.\n\nWait, but the department manager's argument was about reducing training costs by 20% by switching to V. The GM's counter is that they can hire M-skilled employees, thus avoiding the need to train. If option C is true, then hiring M-skilled employees is more expensive, which makes the GM's alternative less attractive. So the GM's opposition is weakened because their alternative isn't as good as they thought.\n\nAlternatively, option B suggests that employees who learn computers are more likely to leave. So if the company relies on hiring M-skilled employees, but those employees are more likely to leave, then the company would have to keep hiring new M-skilled employees, which might not be sustainable. This could make the GM's strategy less effective because it's not a long-term solution.\n\nHmm, I'm a bit torn between B and C. Let me think again.\n\nThe GM's position is that hiring M-skilled employees is a better alternative to switching to V. So, if option C is true, hiring M-skilled employees is more expensive, which makes the GM's alternative less cost-effective. Therefore, the GM's opposition is weakened because their alternative isn't as good as they thought.\n\nOption B, on the other hand, affects the sustainability of the GM's strategy. If employees are more likely to leave after learning, then the company would have to keep hiring new M-skilled employees, which might not be feasible or cost-effective in the long run. So this also weakens the GM's argument because their strategy isn't sustainable.\n\nBetween B and C, which one is more directly related to the cost argument? The GM's argument is about cost (avoiding training costs by hiring M-skilled employees). So if hiring M-skilled employees is more expensive (option C), that directly impacts the cost-effectiveness of the GM's alternative. Therefore, option C would weaken the GM's opposition more.\n\nWait, but the GM's argument is that hiring M-skilled employees is a better alternative, not necessarily that it's cheaper. The GM didn't mention cost, just that it's a better reason. So if option C is true, it makes hiring M-skilled employees more expensive, which would make the GM's alternative less attractive, thus weakening their opposition.\n\nAlternatively, option B affects the sustainability, but perhaps not the cost directly. So I think option C is the correct answer.\n</think>\n\nThe correct answer is:\n\n**C) Employees who are proficient in using m-type computers will demand higher income than those who are expected to have no experience in using computers.**\n\n**Explanation:**\nThe general manager's argument relies on the feasibility and cost-effectiveness of hiring employees who already know how to use M-type computers. If employees proficient in M-type computers demand higher salaries, this directly undermines the GM's position by making the alternative of hiring M-skilled employees more expensive. This weakens the GM's opposition to switching to V-type computers, as the cost-effectiveness of their proposed solution is reduced.",
