      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I've got this logical reasoning problem here, and I need to figure out the correct answer. Let me read through the context and the question carefully.\n\nThe context talks about macroeconomic control policies in my country, which are causing the economy to slow down. Because of this, the demand for oil and natural gas is decreasing, which eases the supply-demand contradiction to some extent. Then there's a forecast from the International Energy Agency. It says that this year, my country's crude oil production will reach 175 million tons, which is a 1% increase from last year. Consumption is expected to exceed 300 million tons, up about 12% from last year. Imports are projected to be over 100 million tons, possibly close to 120 million tons, which is a 30% increase from last year.\n\nThe question is asking which of the options can be inferred from this text. The options are A, B, C, D.\n\nLet me break down each option one by one.\n\nOption A: My country's crude oil supply is tight. Hmm. The context mentions that the demand for oil is decreasing, which eases the supply-demand contradiction. So if demand is decreasing, maybe the supply isn't as tight as before. But wait, the production is only increasing by 1%, while consumption is up 12%. So even though production is up a little, consumption is way up. That might mean that the supply isn't keeping up with demand, making the supply tight. But the context also says that the demand is decreasing because of macroeconomic policies. Wait, that's a bit confusing. Let me think again. The context says that the demand for oil and natural gas will decrease, so the supply-demand contradiction will be eased. But then the IEA forecast says that consumption is increasing by 12%. That seems contradictory. Maybe I'm misunderstanding. Oh, wait, the context says that the demand will decrease, but the IEA says consumption is increasing. That's a bit confusing. Maybe the context is saying that the demand is decreasing in terms of growth, but the actual numbers are still increasing. So perhaps the supply is tight because consumption is rising faster than production. So maybe A is correct.\n\nOption B: My country's crude oil consumption mainly depends on imports. The context says that imports are expected to exceed 100 million tons, possibly close to 120 million tons. Consumption is over 300 million tons. So imports are about 100-120 million tons, while production is 175 million tons. So total supply would be production plus imports, which is 175 + 120 = 295 million tons. Consumption is 300 million tons. So the country is almost meeting its consumption with production and imports, but just barely. So the consumption is mainly met by both production and imports. But the question is whether it mainly depends on imports. Since production is 175 and imports are 120, production is higher than imports. So consumption is mainly from production, not imports. So B might not be correct.\n\nOption C: My country's dependence on imported crude oil has increased. Dependence on imports would be the ratio of imports to total consumption. Last year, let's see. This year's imports are up 30% from last year. Let's calculate last year's imports. If this year is 120 million tons, which is a 30% increase, then last year's imports were 120 / 1.3 \u2248 92.3 million tons. Consumption last year was 300 / 1.12 \u2248 267.86 million tons. So last year's dependence was 92.3 / 267.86 \u2248 34.4%. This year, imports are 120, consumption is 300. So dependence is 120 / 300 = 40%. So yes, dependence has increased from about 34.4% to 40%. So C seems correct.\n\nOption D: My country's demand for energy will become less and less. The context says that the demand for oil and natural gas will decrease, but the IEA forecast shows that consumption is increasing. So this seems contradictory. Maybe the context is saying that the growth in demand is slowing down, but not that the actual demand is decreasing. So D might not be correct because the actual demand is still increasing, just at a slower rate.\n\nSo putting it all together, the correct answer seems to be C. My country's dependence on imported crude oil has increased.\n</think>\n\nThe correct answer is C) My country's dependence on imported crude oil has increased.\n\n**Step-by-Step Explanation:**\n\n1. **Understanding the Context:**\n   - Macroeconomic policies are slowing down economic growth, reducing oil and gas demand, easing supply-demand issues.\n   - IEA forecast: Production up 1% to 175M tons; consumption up 12% to 300M tons; imports up 30% to ~120M tons.\n\n2. **Analyzing Each Option:**\n   - **A) Supply is tight:** Despite production increase, consumption growth is higher, suggesting tight supply. However, context mentions demand decrease, so A is less certain.\n   - **B) Consumption mainly depends on imports:** Imports (120M) < Production (175M), so B is incorrect.\n   - **C) Dependence on imports increased:** Last year's imports \u224892.3M (34.4% of 267.86M consumption). This year, 120M (40% of 300M). Dependence increased, so C is correct.\n   - **D) Demand decreasing:** Consumption is rising, so D is incorrect.\n\n**Answer:** C) My country's dependence on imported crude oil has increased.",
