      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I'm trying to solve this logical reasoning problem. Let me read through the context and question again to make sure I understand it properly.\n\nThe context is about an insurance company, Company X, that's thinking about issuing a new policy. This policy is for elderly people who have diseases that are common among the elderly. The problem they're facing is that the premiums need to be low enough to attract customers, but they're worried that the income from these policies won't cover the claims made. So, they're concerned about potential losses.\n\nThe question is asking which strategy would most likely minimize Company X's losses on these policies. The options are A, B, C, D.\n\nLet me break this down. The company wants to minimize losses, which means they want to ensure that the premiums they collect are enough to cover the claims. Since the premiums are low, they need to manage the risk so that the claims don't exceed the income.\n\nLooking at the options:\n\nOption A: Insuring only those individuals who did not suffer any serious diseases as children. Hmm, that seems a bit off. The policy is for elderly diseases, so past childhood diseases might not be directly relevant. Unless it's implying that people who were healthier as children might be healthier as elderly, but I'm not sure if that's a strong connection. It might not be the best strategy.\n\nOption B: Insuring only those individuals who are wealthy enough to pay for medical services. Wait, if they're wealthy, why would they need insurance? They might not even use the policy much because they can afford their own medical care. But the company is trying to attract customers with low premiums, so targeting wealthy individuals might not be the right approach because they might not buy the policy if they can pay out of pocket. Plus, the company's concern is about claims, so if the wealthy don't use the policy, maybe that's good, but I'm not sure if this is the best option.\n\n