law. Therefore, this doesn't fit the definition because the result could have been avoided.\n\nOption D: D was driving overloaded, and Wang crossed the road. D managed to brake in time and didn't hit Wang. Here, D committed an illegal act by overloading. But since D did manage to avoid hitting Wang, the result (injury) didn't occur. The definition talks about a situation where the result cannot be avoided even if the law is followed. In this case, D's illegal act didn't lead to the result because he was able to brake. So this doesn't fit either because the result was avoided.\n\nWait, maybe I made a mistake with option B. Let me think again. If B was drunk driving, and Wang was also drunk and rushed onto the road. If B hadn't been drunk, maybe he could have reacted better and avoided the collision. But since both were drunk, it's possible that even if B had been sober, the pedestrian's actions might have still caused the injury. So in this case, the result (Wang being injured) couldn't have been avoided even if B had followed the law. That would fit the definition of obligatory alternative action because the illegal act (drunk driving) caused the result, but the result would have happened regardless.\n\nWait, but I'm not entirely sure. Let me compare with the other options. Option C: C was speeding, which is illegal. If he hadn't been speeding, he might have avoided the accident. So the result could have been avoided, so it's not obligatory. Option D: D's illegal act didn't lead to the result because he braked in time. So the result was avoided, so it's not obligatory.\n\nSo the only option that fits is B because even if B hadn't been drunk, the pedestrian's actions might have still caused the injury, making it unavoidable. Therefore, B is the correct answer.\n</think>\n\nThe correct answer is:\n\n**B) B drunk driving, pedestrian wang also drunk, from the sidewalk suddenly rushed to b, wang was injured**\n\n**Explanation:**\nIn this scenario, B committed an illegal 