police are perceived as unresponsive, people might not report thefts. So the actual number of thefts could be higher, but fewer are being reported. That would mean the statistics don't accurately reflect the real crime rate. So this directly challenges the mayor's argument because it suggests that the decrease in reported thefts might not mean there's actually less crime; it could just mean people aren't reporting as much.\n\nOption C: The mayor's critics generally agree that police statistics concerning crime reports provide the most reliable available data on crime rates.\n\nWait, if the critics agree that the statistics are reliable, that actually supports the mayor's argument. Because the mayor is using those statistics to say crime went down. So if the critics agree the data is reliable, it doesn't challenge the mayor's argument; it might even strengthen it because the critics can't argue that the data is flawed.\n\nOption D: The mayor's reorganization of the police department failed to save as much money as it was intended to save.\n\nThis is about the cost-saving aspect, not directly about crime rates. The mayor's argument is about crime decreasing, not about whether they saved money. So even if they didn't save as much money, that doesn't directly challenge the claim that crime went down. It might make the reorganization less successful overall, but it doesn't weaken the argument about crime rates.\n\nSo, putting it all together, the strongest challenge comes from Option B. It directly questions the validity of the statistics the mayor is using by suggesting that the decrease in reported thefts might not reflect an actual decrease in crime but rather a decrease in reporting due to perceived unresponsiveness of the police. That would mean the mayor's conclusion is based on flawed data, which seriously challenges their argument.\n</think>\n\nThe correct answer is:\n\n**B) When city police are perceived as unresponsive, victims of theft are less likely to report thefts to the police.**\n\n**Explanation:**\nThe mayor's argument relies on the decrease in reported thefts as evidence that crime has decreased. However, if the police are perceived as unresponsive, victims may be less likely to report thefts. This means the reported decrease in thefts could be due to underreporting rather than an actual reduction in crime, directly challenging the mayor's conclusion.",
