 Canada. So if it doesn't travel far, maybe it's from local sources. But the ban was in North America, so maybe it's from elsewhere. Hmm, this seems a bit conflicting. If toxaphene doesn't travel far, then it's unlikely to come from other parts of the world, so maybe the high levels are from local sources that continued using it despite the ban. But the context says it's banned in North America, so maybe they stopped. So this option might not explain it as well as B.\n\nOption D: Toxic chemicals become more readily detectable once they enter organisms the size of fish. So, if toxaphene is in fish, it's easier to detect. But the question is about why the water has high levels, not why it's detectable. So this might not directly explain the presence of high levels in the water. It could be a factor in detection, but not the cause of the high levels themselves.\n\nSo, putting it all together, the most plausible explanation is Option B. Even though toxaphene was banned decades ago, it can persist in the environment for a long time, leading to high levels still present now. Option A is possible but less likely because the ban was enforced, and the context says it's only used elsewhere now. Option C seems to contradict the idea of it being present if it doesn't travel far, and Option D doesn't address the cause of the high levels but rather detection.\n</think>\n\nThe correct answer is B) Levels of pesticides in the environment often continue to be high for decades after their use ends.\n\n**Explanation:**\nThe presence of high toxaphene levels in Lake Laberge, despite the pesticide being banned in North America in the early 1980s, is best explained by the persistence of pesticides in the environment. Pesticides can remain in the environment for many years after their use has ceased, which aligns with the observed high levels in the lake. This explanation is more plausible than the other options, which either contradict the context or do not directly address the cause of the high levels. \n\n**Answer:** B",
