nterexample to a theory. I don't think the argument is presenting a counterexample. It's more about evaluating possibilities based on known facts about the planets. So D doesn't seem right.\n\nPutting it all together, the argument starts with possible sources (Mercury, Venus, Mars) and then systematically eliminates Mercury and Venus based on their physical characteristics. This leaves Mars as the only remaining option. That process is about eliminating competing alternatives, which matches option A.\n\nI think I'm confident that the answer is A. The argument uses a process of elimination by considering each possible source and dismissing them based on specific reasons, thus deriving the conclusion that Mars is the source.\n</think>\n\nThe argument systematically eliminates Mercury and Venus as possible sources of the meteorites by considering their unique characteristics, thereby concluding that Mars is the most likely origin. This method of reasoning is best described as eliminating competing alternative explanations.\n\n**Answer: A) Eliminating competing alternative explanations**",
