      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I need to figure out which of the options A, B, C, or D is an example of direct selling based on the given context. Let me start by understanding what direct selling is. The context says that direct selling is when enterprises recruit sellers to sell products directly to final consumers outside of a fixed business place. So, the key points here are that the sellers are recruited by the company, they sell directly to consumers, and this happens outside a fixed location.\n\nNow, let's look at each option one by one.\n\nOption A: A dairy manufacturer sets up 100 sales points in the city to sell products at a unified price. Hmm, so they have sales points, which are fixed locations. The context says direct selling happens outside fixed business places, so this might not be direct selling. It sounds more like a retail setup with fixed points.\n\nOption B: A bookstore uses online sales. Customers order online, and couriers deliver the products. This is e-commerce, right? The sellers here are the bookstore, but the delivery is done by couriers. I'm not sure if this counts as direct selling because the sellers aren't directly interacting with the customers; it's more of a remote sale with delivery involved.\n\nOption C: A cosmetics brand recruits salesmen who set up stalls along the road. They distribute product descriptions and sell at lower prices. This fits because the salesmen are recruited by the company, they're selling directly to consumers, and it's outside a fixed business place. The stalls are temporary or mobile, so that aligns with the context.\n\nOption D: A new student buys five table lamps in a wholesale market. He uses one and sells the other four to classmates. This seems like a personal sale, not a company recruiting sellers. It's more of a secondary sale by an individual, not a direct selling business model.\n\nSo, putting it all together, Option C is the best fit because it involves recruited sellers selling directly to consumers outside a fixed location, which matches the definition of direct selling.\n</think>\n\nThe correct answer is:\n\n**C) Some salesmen recruited by a cosmetics brand set up stalls along the road to distribute product descriptions and sell them to customers at lower prices.**\n\n**Explanation:**\nDirect selling involves recruiting sellers to sell products directly to consumers outside fixed business locations. Option C fits this definition as the salesmen are recruited by the brand and sell directly to customers at temporary stalls.",
