      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I need to figure out which of the options A, B, C, or D is an example of an analogical argument based on the given definition. Let me start by understanding what an analogical argument is. From the context, it says that an analogical argument is based on the sameness or similarity of two objects in some attributes and then infers that they are similar in other attributes. The logical form is that object A has attributes a, b, c, d, and object B has a, b, c, so B must have d.\n\nSo, I need to look for an option where two things are compared based on some shared attributes, and then it's inferred that they share another attribute. Let me go through each option one by one.\n\nOption A: \"Tao yuanming does not bow for five buckets of rice, li bai does not destroy eyebrows and bend over power. this shows that ambitious people are not proud of high officials and generous salaries.\" Hmm, this seems to be using examples of two people who didn't bow to authority or high salaries to generalize about ambitious people. It's more of an inductive argument, using specific instances to make a general point, rather than comparing two objects for similarity in attributes.\n\nOption B: \"Both light and sound have the phenomena of linear propagation, refraction and reflection, so light, like sound, also has fluctuation.\" Okay, here both light and sound are being compared. They share attributes like linear propagation, refraction, and reflection. Then it's inferred that light also has fluctuation, just like sound. That fits the definition because they're similar in some attributes, so they're likely similar in another.\n\nOption C: \"Compared with some people who are irresponsible in their work and afraid of heavy work, comrade bethune is extremely responsible for his work and extremely enthusiastic towards his people, which shows that comrade bethune has a great spirit of being unselfish and dedicated to others.\" This is comparing Bethune to others who are irresponsible. It's highlighting Bethune's positive attributes by contrasting with others, but it's more of a contrastive argument rather than an analogy based on shared attributes.\n\nOption D: \"Silver and copper are both metals with electrical conductivity, but the resistivity of silver is lower, so silver is more suitable to be a conductive material than copper.\" Here, silver and copper are both metals with conductivity, but the argument is about their resistivity. It's more of a comparative argument based on a specific attribute (resistivity) rather than inferring a new attribute based on shared ones.\n\nSo, putting it all together, option B fits the analogical argument because it's comparing two things (light and sound) based on shared attributes and then inferring another shared attribute (fluctuation). The others either use induction, contrast, or comparison based on a single attribute.\n</think>\n\nThe correct answer is:\n\n**B) Both light and sound have the phenomena of linear propagation, refraction and reflection, so light, like sound, also has fluctuation.**\n\n**Explanation:**\nThis option fits the definition of an analogical argument because it compares two objects (light and sound) based on shared attributes (linear propagation, refraction, reflection) and infers another shared attribute (fluctuation).",
