      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I need to figure out which argumentative strategy Judith is using in her reply to Anthony. Let me start by understanding the context.\n\nAnthony is saying that over 80% of heroin users have a history of using marijuana, which he thinks proves that marijuana leads to heroin use. Judith responds by saying that while marijuana might lead to heroin, the statistics alone don't prove it. She then gives an example: 100% of people who take up heroin had a previous history of drinking water. \n\nHmm, so Judith is pointing out that just because a large percentage of heroin users have used something else (in this case, water) doesn't mean that the something else causes the heroin use. She's using a similar structure to Anthony's argument but with an obviously unrelated example to show that the reasoning is flawed.\n\nLooking at the options:\n\nA) She's not saying the statistics are wrong, just that they don't prove causation. So A is probably not it.\n\nB) She's not talking about safety, so B doesn't fit.\n\nC) She's showing that Anthony's conclusion could lead to an absurd consequence, like water causing heroin use. So this might be it.\n\nD) She's demonstrating that Anthony's reasoning is flawed by showing it leads to a false conclusion. That sounds similar to C, but D is more about the reasoning leading to a false conclusion, not just the conclusion itself.\n\nWait, so Judith is using a reductio ad absurdum approach. She's taking Anthony's reasoning and applying it to a ridiculous example to show that the reasoning is flawed. So that would be D, because she's showing that the same reasoning leads to a clearly false conclusion, thus undermining the argument's validity.\n\nAlternatively, C says she's showing that the conclusion is a statement from which absurd consequences can be derived. But actually, she's not attacking the conclusion directly but the reasoning behind it. So D seems more accurate because she's pointing out the flaw in the reasoning by showing it leads to a false conclusion.\n</think>\n\nJudith's argumentative strategy is to demonstrate that Anthony's reasoning is flawed by showing that applying the same logic leads to a clearly false conclusion. \n\n**Answer: D**",
