      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I have this logical reasoning problem to solve. Let me read through the context and the question carefully. \n\nThe context talks about people who know health knowledge but lack self-discipline. It gives examples like wanting to go to bed early but staying up late, knowing smoking and drinking are bad but not resisting, and knowing exercise is good but not doing it. It then says that people with self-discipline go to bed early, avoid certain foods, and keep exercising. If someone adheres to self-discipline, they won't indulge themselves and can maintain their circadian rhythm, leading to a healthy and happy life.\n\nThe question is asking which of the options can be obtained based on the statement. The options are A, B, C, D.\n\nLet me go through each option one by one.\n\nOption A: All people who stick to sports are very disciplined. Hmm, the context says that people with self-discipline stick to sports, but does it say that all who stick to sports are disciplined? Not necessarily. Maybe some people stick to sports for other reasons, like enjoyment, not necessarily because they're disciplined. So A might not be correct.\n\nOption B: Some people who lack self-discipline do not lack health knowledge. The context says that many people are not lacking in health knowledge but lack self-discipline. So it implies that some people who lack self-discipline actually have health knowledge. So B seems correct because it's stating that some of them don't lack health knowledge, which aligns with the context.\n\nOption C: If a person does not adhere to the spirit of self-discipline, he will indulge himself. The context says that if a person adheres to self-discipline, he won't indulge. But does it say that not adhering leads to indulging? It's a bit of a contrapositive. The original statement is \"if adhere, then not indulge.\" The contrapositive would be \"if indulge, then not adhere.\" But the option is stating \"if not adhere, then indulge,\" w