nage and planting of poplars turned tidal wetlands into woodland where rats can live. This changes the habitat, making it more suitable for rats, so more rats. So this supports the expert's point.\n\nWait, but the question is which option does NOT support the expert's view. So I need to find the one that doesn't relate to ecological destruction causing the rodent problem.\n\nWait, option C talks about the water level not rising much last year due to drought, so rats didn't drown. So this year, when the water rose, there were more rats because last year's drought allowed them to survive and multiply. So this is about natural conditions (drought and flood) rather than human-induced ecological destruction. The expert's point is about the destruction of the environment, so option C is about natural factors, not human destruction. Therefore, option C doesn't support the expert's view because it's not about ecological destruction but natural causes.\n\nWait, but the expert says the ecological environment has been destroyed, so the problem is due to human activities. So if option C is about natural factors (drought), then it doesn't support the expert's point that it's due to ecological destruction. So the correct answer would be C because it's a natural cause, not an ecological destruction cause.\n\nWait, but the question is which option does NOT support the expert's point. So if the expert says it's because of ecological destruction, then an option that provides a reason not related to that (like natural causes) would not support the expert's view.\n\nSo, the answer should be C.\n</think>\n\nThe correct answer is:\n\n**C) When the water level of Dongting Lake rises in the flood season every year, many rats can be drowned. However, there was a severe drought last year and the water level did not rise much in the flood season.**\n\n**Explanation:**\nThe expert's viewpoint attributes the rodent disaster to ecological destruction caused by human activities. Option C discusses natural factors (drought and flood) rather than human-induced ecological changes, thus not supporting the expert's argument.",
