      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I have this logical reasoning problem to solve. Let me read through it carefully and try to understand what's being asked.\n\nThe context is about a researcher who went back to a site to recover armadillos they had tagged the previous spring. They found that most of the recaptured armadillos were within a few hundred yards of where they were tagged. From this, the researcher concluded that armadillos don't move quickly into new territories.\n\nThe question is asking which assumption is required by the researcher's argument. The options are A, B, C, or D.\n\nHmm, so I need to figure out what assumption the researcher is making to draw that conclusion. Let's break it down.\n\nThe researcher's conclusion is that armadillos don't move rapidly into new territories. The evidence is that most recaptured armadillos were near their original tagging sites. So, the reasoning is that if they had moved into new territories, they wouldn't be found near the original site. Therefore, since they were found near the original site, they didn't move much.\n\nBut for this conclusion to hold, there must be some underlying assumptions. Let's look at each option.\n\nOption A: A large majority of the recaptured armadillos did not move to a new territory in the intervening summer and then move back to the old territory by the fall.\n\nWait, so this is saying that the armadillos didn't move away and then come back. If they had moved away and then returned, the researcher's conclusion that they don't move into new territories might be incorrect because they could have moved but came back. So the researcher assumes that the armadillos didn't do that. So this could be an assumption.\n\nOption B: Predators did not kill any of the tagged armadillos.\n\nIf predators killed the tagged armadillos, then the researcher wouldn't have recaptured them. But the researcher is only concerned with the ones they did recapture. So even if some were killed, the ones that were recaptured were found near the site. So this might not be a necessary assumption because the argument is about the ones that were recaptured, not about the ones that weren't. So maybe B isn't required.\n\nOption C: The tags identifying the armadillos cannot be removed by the armadillos, either by accident or deliberately.\n\nIf the tags could be removed, then the researcher might have tagged some armadillos that later lost their tags and moved away. But since the researcher is only talking about the ones they recaptured, which still had tags, maybe this isn't an assumption. Because even if some tags were removed, the ones recaptured still had them, so their movement isn't affected. So perhaps C isn't necessary.\n\nOption D: Of the armadillos living in the area of the tagging site last spring, few were able to avoid being tagged by the researcher.\n\nThis is about whether the researcher tagged most of the armadillos in the area. If many armadillos avoided being tagged, then the recaptured ones might not represent the whole population. But the researcher's conclusion is about armadillos in general, not just the tagged ones. Wait, no, the researcher is only talking about the tagged ones. So if many armadillos weren't tagged, the researcher's conclusion might not apply to all armadillos, but the argument is about the tagged ones. So maybe D isn't an assumption because the conclusion is about the tagged armadillos, not the entire population.\n\nWait, but the researcher's conclusion is about armadillos in general, right? Because they say \"armadillos do not move rapidly into new territories.\" So if the researcher only tagged a small subset, maybe the conclusion doesn't hold. But the question is about the assumption required for the argument, not whether the conclusion is valid in general.\n\nWait, no, the argument is based on the recaptured armadillos, so the assumption is that the recaptured ones are representative. But the options don't mention that. So perhaps D is about