nd then came back. So they did move into new territories but returned. So the researcher's conclusion would be wrong because they did move, but just came back. So the assumption is that they didn't move away and come back, which is option A.\n\n2. Maybe the tags fell off, so the armadillos that moved didn't have tags anymore, so the researcher didn't recapture them. So the researcher's conclusion would be based on the ones that didn't move, but the ones that moved were not recaptured because they lost their tags. So the researcher assumes that the tags stayed on, which is option C.\n\nSo both A and C seem like possible assumptions. But which one is required?\n\nThe question is asking for the assumption required by the argument. So the argument is that because the recaptured ones were near the site, they didn't move. So the argument assumes that the recaptured ones didn't move away and come back (A) and that the tags stayed on (C). But which one is more directly required?\n\nI think the main assumption is that the tags stayed on, because if they didn't, the researcher might have missed the ones that moved. So the conclusion relies on the tags being intact. So C is an assumption.\n\nWait, but the researcher is only talking about the ones they recaptured. So if the tags fell off, the researcher wouldn't have recaptured those armadillos, but the ones they did recapture still had tags. So the researcher's conclusion is about the ones that were recaptured, which still had tags. So maybe the assumption is that the tags didn't fall off, so that the recaptured ones are a valid sample. So C is an assumption.\n\nAlternatively, if the armadillos moved away and came back, then the researcher's conclusion that they don't move into new territories would be wrong because they did move. So the assumption is that they didn't move away and come back, which is A.\n\nHmm, I'm a bit stuck. Let me try to think of it another way.\n\nThe researcher's conclusion is that armadillos don't move