      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I have this logical reasoning problem to solve. Let me try to break it down step by step. The context given is: \"Most people who drink too much will have a headache. If Xiao Wang doesn't drink too much, he probably won't have a headache.\" Then the question is asking which of the options A, B, C, or D is similar to this reasoning.\n\nFirst, I need to understand the structure of the reasoning in the context. It seems like it's using a form of probabilistic reasoning. The first statement is a general rule: most people who do X (drink too much) experience Y (headache). The second statement is applying this rule in reverse: if someone doesn't do X (doesn't drink too much), then they probably won't experience Y (headache). So it's like saying, if most X lead to Y, then not X probably leads to not Y.\n\nNow, I need to look at each option and see which one follows the same structure. Let's go through them one by one.\n\nOption A: \"Most greyhounds are easy to train, so Xiao Wang must have no problem training his new dog.\" Here, the reasoning is that because most greyhounds are easy to train, Xiao Wang's dog (a greyhound) will also be easy to train. This is a straightforward application of the general rule to a specific case. It's more of a direct implication rather than the reverse reasoning in the context. So this might not be the same structure.\n\nOption B: \"Most domestic cars are very poor, this car is well done, so it may not be domestic.\" This seems similar to the context. The first part states that most domestic cars are poor. Then, if a car is well done (not poor), it's inferred that it may not be domestic. So it's using the same kind of reverse reasoning: if most X are Y, then if something is not Y, it's probably not X. That matches the structure of the context.\n\nOption C: \"Most famous actors perform very well, because Lao Gao is not a famous actor, so he may not act well.\" This is similar to the context. The first part say