      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I need to figure out Gamba's strategy in arguing against Munoz. Let me read through the context again to make sure I understand it correctly.\n\nMunoz says that the Southwest Hopeville Neighbors Association (SHNA) overwhelmingly opposes the new water system, and he uses this as evidence that the whole city opposes it. But Gamba is countering that argument. \n\nFrom the context, I gather that the SHNA did pass a resolution opposing the system, but only 25 out of 350 members voted. Out of those 25, 10 were in favor, so 15 opposed. Gamba points out that 15 opposing votes are way less than 1% of the city's population. He argues that you can't assume such a small number of votes represents the majority view of the city.\n\nSo, Gamba is challenging Munoz's conclusion that citywide opposition exists based on the SHNA's resolution. He's pointing out that the sample size is too small\u2014only 25 voters out of 350 members, and even fewer in the context of the entire city.\n\nLooking at the options:\n\nA) Criticizing a view based on evidence that's impossible to disconfirm. Hmm, not sure. The evidence here is the vote, which is specific and can be checked, so maybe not A.\n\nB) Casting doubt on the conclusion by saying the statistical sample is too small. That sounds like what Gamba is doing. He's saying 25 voters, especially 15 opposing, don't represent the whole city because it's a small sample.\n\nC) Refuting by showing premises don't guarantee the conclusion. That's a bit more about logical structure. Gamba is more about the sample size, so maybe not C.\n\nD) Questioning based on voting likelihood\u2014like certain people are more likely to vote. The context doesn't mention anything about voter demographics or likelihood, just the small number. So probably not D.\n\nSo, the best fit is B. Gamba is arguing that the sample size is too small to be reliable, thus doubting the conclusion of citywide opposition.\n</think>\n\nGamba's strategy involves pointing out that the statistical sample used by Munoz is too small to reliably represent the city's opinion. He argues that the 15 opposing votes from a small group of 25 voters cannot accurately reflect the majority view of the entire city.\n\n**Answer: B**",
