      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I've got this logical reasoning problem here, and I need to figure out the correct answer. Let me read through it carefully.\n\nThe context is about Innovair, an airline company, trying to reduce ground collisions. They're planning to give their ground crews additional training, which will cost $500,000 annually. The training is expected to cut the number of ground collisions by about half. However, the drop in repair costs is expected to be much greater. The question is asking which option logically completes the argument.\n\nThe question provides some specific data: in a typical year, Innovair has 35 collisions while parked or being towed, costing $1,000,000 in repairs. So, the current repair cost is $1 million per year.\n\nThe argument is that even though the number of collisions will only be reduced by half (so from 35 to about 17 or 18), the repair costs will drop much more significantly. So, the reasoning must be that the remaining collisions after the training are less costly, or that the training is targeting the most expensive collisions.\n\nLooking at the options:\n\nOption A: Most ground collisions have been caused by the least experienced ground-crew members. Hmm, this suggests that training would reduce collisions by improving the least experienced crew. But the argument is about repair costs, not just the number of collisions. So, if the number of collisions is halved, but the repair costs drop more, maybe because the remaining collisions are less severe. So A might not directly address the repair cost aspect.\n\nOption B: A ground collision typically occurs when there are no passengers on the airplane. If collisions happen when there are no passengers, maybe the damage is less because the plane isn't as full, but I'm not sure how that ties into the repair costs. Maybe without passengers, the plane is lighter, so damage is less? Or perhaps it's about liability, but the question is about repair costs, so I'm not sure if B is the right fit.\n\nOption C: The additional training will focus on helping ground crews avoid those kinds of ground collisions that cause the most costly damage. This seems relevant because if the training targets the most expensive collisions, then even if the number of collisions is only halved, the repair costs could drop more because the expensive ones are being avoided. So, for example, if some collisions cause $50,000 in damage and others cause $100,000, focusing on the latter would reduce the overall repair costs more than just halving the number.\n\nOption D: The $500,000 cost figure includes the wages during training. This seems more about the cost of training rather than the repair costs. It doesn't directly explain why repair costs would drop more than the number of collisions.\n\nSo, putting it together, the argument is that even though collisions are only reduced by half, repair costs drop more. The reason must be that the training is focusing on preventing the most expensive collisions. Therefore, option C makes the most sense because it explains why the repair costs would decrease disproportionately.\n</think>\n\nThe correct answer is:\n\n**C) The additional training will focus on helping ground crews avoid those kinds of ground collisions that cause the most costly damage**\n\n**Explanation:**\nThe argument hinges on why repair costs would drop more than the number of collisions. Option C explains that the training targets the most expensive collisions, leading to a greater reduction in repair costs despite only halving the collision count.",
