is true, A must be false, which makes B true, which contradicts the condition that only one statement is true. So, C cannot be the true statement.\n\n4. Suppose D is true. So, D's statement: \"There are letters in the third drawer.\" That means the third drawer has letters. If D is the only true statement, then A, B, and C must be false.\n\n   - A's statement is false: \"There are no books in some drawers.\" So, all drawers have books.\n   \n   - B's statement is false: \"There are books in all drawers.\" Wait, again, if A is false, then all drawers have books, making B's statement true. But we assumed only D is true, so this is a contradiction. Therefore, D cannot be the only true statement because it would require B to be true as well.\n\nSo, from the above analysis, the only possible scenarios where only one statement is true are when either A or B is true. Now, let's see what each scenario implies about the options given.\n\nIf A is true:\n- Some drawers have no books.\n- The second drawer has a pen (since C is false).\n- The third drawer has no letters (since D is false).\n\nIf B is true:\n- All drawers have books.\n- The second drawer has a pen (since C is false).\n- The third drawer has no letters (since D is false).\n\nNow, let's look at the options:\n\nOption A: There is a pen in the second drawer.\nIn both scenarios where A or B is true, the second drawer has a pen because C's statement is false. So, this must be true.\n\nOption B: There are letters in the third drawer.\nIn both scenarios, D's statement is false, so the third drawer has no letters. Therefore, this is false.\n\nOption C: There are books in all four drawers.\nThis is true only if B is the true statement. If A is true, then not all drawers have books. So, this isn't necessarily true.\n\nOption D: There are no books in the four drawers.\nThis would mean all drawers have no books, which contradicts A's statement because A says some have no books, not all. So, this isn't necessarily true.\n\nWait, but let me double-check. If A is true, then some drawers have no books, but others might have books. So, it's possible that some have books and some don't. Therefore, option C (\"all have books\") is only true if B is the true statement. But since both A and B could be the true statements, we can't be certain that option C is true. Similarly, option D (\"no books in any\") is not necessarily true because A only says some have no books, not all.\n\nBut wait, in the scenario where A is true, does that mean that at least one drawer has no books, but others could have books. So, the second drawer has a pen, but it could also have books or not. The third drawer has no letters, but it could have books or not. The first and fourth drawers could have books or not, as long as at least one has no books.\n\nHowever, the key point is that in both scenarios (A true or B true), the second drawer has a pen. Therefore, option A must be true.\n\nWait, but let me think again. If A is true, then some drawers have no books. But the second drawer has a pen, but it could also have books or not. The third drawer has no letters, but it could have books or not. The first and fourth drawers could have books or not, as long as at least one has no books.\n\nBut the question is asking which of the options must be true. So, regardless of whether A or B is the true statement, the second drawer has a pen because in both cases, C's statement is false. Therefore, option A must be true.\n\nWait, but let me make sure. If A is true, then some drawers have no books. But the second drawer has a pen, but it could also have books or not. The third drawer has no letters, but it could have books or not. The first and fourth drawers could have books or not, as long as at least one has no books.\n\nBut the question is about which option must be true. So, looking at the options:\n\nA) There is a pen in the second drawer. This is true in both scenarios where A or B is the true statement because C's statement is false in both cases.\n\nB) There are letters in the third drawer. This is false in both scenarios because D's statement is false.\n\nC) There are books in all four drawers. This is only true if B is the true statement. If A is true, then not all drawers have books, so this isn't necessarily true.\n\nD) There are no books in the four drawers. This would mean all drawers have no books, which contradicts A's statement because A says some have no books, not all. So, this isn't necessarily true.\n\nTherefore, the only option that must be true is A.\n</think>\n\nThe correct answer is:\n\n**A) There is a pen in the second drawer.**\n\n**Step-by-Step Explanation:**\n\n1. **Identify the Statements:**\n   - A: Some drawers have no books.\n   - B: All drawers have books.\n   - C: Second drawer has no pen.\n   - D: Third drawer has letters.\n\n2. **Assume Only One Statement is True:**\n   - If A is true, then some drawers have no books. This makes B false (not all have books). C must be false, so the second drawer has a pen. D is false, so the third drawer has no letters.\n   - If B is true, all drawers have books. This makes A false. C is false, so the second drawer has a pen. D is false, so the third drawer has no letters.\n\n3. **Evaluate Options:**\n   - **A:** True in both scenarios because C is false.\n   - **B:** False because D is false.\n   - **C:** True only if B is true, but not necessarily always.\n   - **D:** False because it contradicts A's statement.\n\n4. **Conclusion:**\n   - Only option A must be true regardless of whether A or B is the true statement.\n\n**Answer:** A) There is a pen in the second drawer.",
