me think about the structure of the argument. The argument is that higher taxes lead to higher prices, which lead to fewer buyers. So, any factor that affects either the price elasticity of demand (how much demand changes with price) or the availability of alternatives would weaken the conclusion.\n\nOption B affects the demand elasticity\u2014people are less responsive to price changes because of higher income. Option D affects the supply\u2014more alternatives available, so even if taxed cigarettes are more expensive, people can get them elsewhere.\n\nI think both are correct, but perhaps the intended answer is D because it's about the effectiveness of the tax in actually reducing consumption, considering that people can switch to other sources. Alternatively, B is about the demand side.\n\nWait, the question is about which option, if true, weakens the conclusion. So, both B and D are correct, but perhaps the answer is B because it's more directly about the effect on the number of people buying cigarettes. Alternatively, D is about the supply side, which is also relevant.\n\nI'm a bit confused, but I think the correct answer is B because it directly affects the demand. If people's income increases, they are less sensitive to price, so the tax increase doesn't reduce the number of buyers as much. That's a direct weakening of the conclusion.\n\nWait, but the context says that the tax increase is expected to increase the price, which will reduce the number of buyers. So, if people's income has increased, they are less sensitive to the price increase, meaning the number of buyers doesn't decrease as much. That's a direct counter.\n\nOption D is about the supply side\u2014more private cigarettes. So, even if the price of taxed cigarettes goes up, people can buy cheaper, untaxed ones, so overall consumption doesn't decrease. That's also a counter.\n\nI think both are correct, but perhaps the intended answer is B because it's more about the demand side, which is what the