which might mean they can spend more later. But how does this affect Li Ming's argument? Li Ming says unemployed can't spend more. If non-unemployed are saving because prices are low, maybe they can spend more when prices are low, which would help the economy improve, supporting Zhang Qiang. So this could weaken Li Ming's point because it shows that even if some are unemployed, others can spend more when prices drop.\n\nWait, but Li Ming's argument is specifically about the unemployed not being able to increase consumption. So if the non-unemployed are able to save and spend more when prices are low, that could still lead to increased consumption and economic improvement, which would weaken Li Ming's argument because Li Ming is saying that because people are unemployed, they can't increase consumption, but if others can, then the economy can still improve.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I'm overcomplicating. Let me think again.\n\nLi Ming's main point is that when people lose jobs, they can't increase consumption beyond necessities. So even if prices are low, they can't buy more. So to weaken this, we need a scenario where even unemployed people can increase consumption when prices are low.\n\nLooking at the options, none directly say that. Option C says they still buy necessities, but that's not increasing consumption beyond that. Option D talks about non-unemployed saving and then spending when prices are low, which would help the economy, but it doesn't directly address the unemployed.\n\nWait, maybe I'm missing something. Let me read the options again.\n\nOption D: In times of economic deterioration, even those who are not unemployed will cut back on spending; when prices hit rock bottom, they will have money to save for consumption.\n\nSo when prices are low, non-unemployed have more money to save, which they can then use for consumption. So even if some are unemployed, others can spend more because they have saved money. This would mean that overall consumption can increase, which would support Zhang Qiang's argument that low prices lead to increased consumption and economic improvement. Therefore, this weakens Li Ming's argument because Li Ming was saying that because people are unemployed, they can't increase consumption, but if others can, then the economy can still improve.\n\nSo the correct answer would be D.\n</think>\n\nThe correct answer is D. This option weakens Li Ming's argument by showing that even if some are unemployed, others can increase their consumption when prices drop, leading to economic improvement.\n\n**Answer: D**",
