      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I need to figure out which option best explains the phenomenon described in the context. Let me read through the context again to make sure I understand it correctly.\n\nThe context says that parents are paying more attention to their children's reading. In 2018, 70.3% of families in a city had the habit of parents reading with their children. The amount of books read by parents and children increased by 1.8 percentage points compared to 2017, and the length of the books also increased. However, despite this, the number of books read per child in 2018 was 4.72, which is down by 0.6 percentage points from 2017.\n\nSo, the phenomenon here is that even though more families are reading together, and the amount and length of books are increasing, the average number of books read per child is actually decreasing. That seems a bit contradictory. I need to find an option that explains why this is happening.\n\nLet me look at the options one by one.\n\nOption A: In recent years, children have a heavy burden of schoolwork and many people want to read extracurricular reading but do not have enough time.\n\nHmm, this suggests that children are too busy with schoolwork to read more. But wait, the context says that the amount of books read by parents and children increased. So if they're reading more, even if it's just a bit, why would the average per child decrease? Maybe because the number of children is increasing, but that's not mentioned. Alternatively, maybe the time spent on reading is being taken away by schoolwork, but the context says the amount and length increased, so perhaps this isn't the best explanation.\n\nOption B: Most parents born in the 1980s and 1990s are highly educated and value reading for their children.\n\nThis seems to support the idea that parents are more involved in reading, which aligns with the increase in reading habits. But it doesn't explain why the number of books per child is decreasing. In fact, if parents value reading more, we'd expect the number of books to increase, not decrease. So this might not be the right answer.\n\nOption C: Many children are used to listening to books in parent-child reading, which is not counted in 2018.\n\nOh, this is interesting. If children are listening to books (like audiobooks) during parent-child reading sessions, but the 2018 statistics only count the books that children read themselves, not the ones they listen to, then the actual engagement with books is higher. But the count of books read by the children themselves might be lower because they're just listening. So the number of books they physically read could be decreasing, even though they're still consuming more content through listening. That would explain why the amount and length increased (because they're reading more with parents, possibly longer books), but the count of books read by the children themselves decreased.\n\nOption D: Electronic reading by parents on mobile phones and computers has not been taken into account in children's books.\n\nThis suggests that parents are reading electronically, but it's not counted in the children's books. Wait, the context says the amount of books read by parents and children increased. So if parents are reading more electronically, maybe that's contributing to the increase. But how does that affect the children's count? If the children's books are only counting physical books, and parents are reading electronically, maybe the children aren't reading as many physical books, hence the decrease. But I'm not sure if this directly explains the phenomenon as well as option C does.\n\nLet me think again. The key point is that while the amount and length of books read by parents and children increased, the number of books read per child decreased. So the children are reading fewer books themselves, but the overall reading (including parents) is up. So perhaps the children are not reading as many books on their own, but the parents are reading more with them, possibly longer books, which might include listening rather than reading.\n\nOption C says that children are listening to books during parent-child reading, which isn't counted. So if the children are just listening, they aren't actually reading the books themselves, so their count doesn't go up. Meanwhile, the parents are reading more, which increases the overall amount and length. That makes sense because the children's own reading count would decrease if they're just listening instead of reading.\n\nOption D is about electronic reading by parents not being counted in children's books. If parents are reading electronically, maybe the children aren't reading as much because the parents are reading instead, but I'm not sure if that's the main factor here. It's possible, but I think option C is a better fit because it directly relates to the children's reading habits.\n\nSo, putting it all together, the best explanation is option C: Many children are used to listening to books in parent-child reading, which is not counted in 2018.\n</think>\n\nThe phenomenon described is that while more families are reading together and the amount and length of books read are increasing, the average number of books read per child is decreasing. This suggests that children are engaging with books in a way that isn't captured by the reading count, such as listening to books during parent-child reading sessions. This explains why the overall reading metrics are up, but the children's individual book count is down.\n\n**Answer: C**",
