oser to the overall average. So, if there are more physical therapists, their lower average would bring the overall average down closer to 40k.\n\nSo, looking at the options:\n\nA) There was no dietician at Happywell last year who earned less than the average for a physical therapist.\n\nHmm, the average for physical therapists is 42k, so this would mean that all dieticians earned at least 42k. But the dietician's average is 50k, which is higher, so it's possible that some dieticians earned more than 42k, but it's not necessarily true that none earned less. For example, some could have earned 45k, which is above 42k, but others could have earned 55k. So, this conclusion isn't necessarily true.\n\nB) At least one Happywell employee earned less than the average for a physical therapist last year.\n\nThe average for physical therapists is 42k, so if the overall average is 40k, which is lower, that suggests that some employees earned less than 42k. Since the overall average is lower, there must be employees earning below 42k. Therefore, this conclusion is correct.\n\nC) At least one dietician earned more than the highest-paid Happywell physical therapist last year.\n\nThis would mean that the maximum salary of a dietician is higher than the maximum salary of a physical therapist. But we don't have information about individual salaries, only averages. So, this conclusion can't be drawn.\n\nD) There were more physical therapists than dieticians at Happywell last year.\n\nAs I thought earlier, since the overall average is closer to the physical therapists' average, there must be more physical therapists than dieticians. So, this conclusion is also correct.\n\nWait, but the question is asking which conclusion can be properly drawn. So, both B and D seem correct. But let me check again.\n\nWait, the overall average is 40k, which is lower than both 42k and 50k. That can't happen unless there are other employees with salaries below 40k. So, the conclusion B is correct because at least one employee earned less than 42k, which is the physical therapists' average. But the problem is, the overall average is 40k, so the average of all employees is 40k, which is lower than 42k, so at least some employees must have earned less than 42k. Therefore, B is correct.\n\nAs for D, if the only employees were dieticians and physical therapists, the overall average would have to be between 42k and 50k, but it's 40k, which is lower. Therefore, there must be other employees with lower salaries. So, the number of physical therapists compared to dieticians isn't necessarily more because the presence of other employees could affect the overall average. Therefore, D might not be necessarily correct because the number of physical therapists could be the same as dieticians, but the presence of other low earners brings the average down.\n\nWait, but the problem doesn't mention other employees, so perhaps the question is only considering dieticians and physical therapists. In that case, the overall average can't be lower than both, so the only way is if there are more physical therapists. Therefore, D would be correct.\n\nBut I'm getting confused because the overall average is lower than both groups, which is impossible unless there are other employees. So, the problem must include other employees, but the question is about dieticians and physical therapists. Therefore, the conclusion that can be drawn is that there are more physical therapists than dieticians because their average is closer to the overall average, but I'm not sure.\n\nWait, let me think again. If the overall average is 40k, and the average for dieticians is 50k, and for physical therapists is 42k, then the number of physical therapists must be more than dieticians to bring the overall average down. Because 42k is closer to 40k than 50k is. So, the more physical therapists, the closer the overall average would be to 42k. Therefore, D is correct.\n\nBut also, since the overall average is 40k, which