 based on their classifications, leading to false memories. So this option seems unrelated.\n\nNow, between A and B. The context specifically states that older children are more likely to have false memories. So A is directly supported. B talks about different mistakes, which is also true, but the question is asking which conforms to the context. The context's main point is about the likelihood of false memories, so A is the best answer.\n\nWait, but the context says older participants were more likely to develop different types of false memories. So it's not just that they make mistakes, but that they have more false memories. So A is correct because it directly states that older children are more likely to have false memories than younger ones.\n\nI think I'm confident with that. So the answer should be A.\n</think>\n\nThe correct answer is:\n\n**A) Older children are more likely to have false memories than younger ones**\n\n**Explanation:**\nThe context explicitly states that older participants were more likely to develop different types of false memories than younger ones due to having more classifications in their minds. This directly supports option A. While option B is also true, it is less specific than A, which aligns more closely with the main point of the context. Options C and D are not supported by the provided information.",
