 fewer hours because they reach their target quickly. So when the effective hourly wage is high, they work less. That contradicts option C, which says they work longer when the effective wage is high. So the facts here actually go against C. So C is a possible answer.\n\nOption D: \"Workers will accept a lower hourly wage in exchange for the freedom to set their own schedules.\" The context doesn't mention anything about accepting lower wages. It's about setting a target and stopping when they reach it, which gives them control over their schedule. But the question is whether they accept lower wages for that freedom. The context doesn't say anything about their wages being lower; it's about their income target. So I don't think the facts here address D. So D is probably not the answer.\n\nWait, let me double-check. The question is which option is contradicted by the facts. So the facts show that when the effective hourly wage is high (busy days), they work fewer hours. So option C says they work longer when the effective wage is high, which is the opposite. So the facts provide evidence against C.\n\nSo the answer should be C.\n</think>\n\nThe facts provided indicate that taxi drivers work fewer hours on busy days when their effective hourly wage is higher, contradicting the idea that people work longer when their effective hourly wage is high. Therefore, the correct answer is:\n\n**C) People work longer when their effective hourly wage is high than when it is low.**\n\n**Answer: C**",
