ed.\" The context mentions that these everyday events (like human error or plumbing issues) can't be thought unlikely over the long run. But does that mean the number of accidents will increase every year? Not necessarily. It just says they are not unlikely, so we might expect some accidents, but not necessarily an ever-increasing number. So B might be overstating the case.\n\nOption C: \"The design of nuclear power plants attempts to compensate for possible failures of the materials used in their construction.\" The context doesn't mention anything about the design of the plants or how they compensate for material failures. It's talking about the causes of accidents, not the design measures. So C doesn't seem supported by the context.\n\nOption D: \"If nuclear power plants continue in operation, a serious accident at such a plant is not improbable.\" The context says that these everyday events (human error, plumbing) can't be considered unlikely over time. So if plants keep operating, it's not improbable that such accidents will happen again. This seems to align with the context because it's pointing out that these causes are not rare and can be expected over the long run.\n\nSo, putting it all together, the context supports the idea that serious accidents are not unlikely if plants continue operating, which is what D states. A is too definitive about the future, B is about increasing incidents which isn't supported, and C is about design which isn't mentioned. Therefore, D is the most strongly supported option.\n</think>\n\nThe context discusses that serious accidents at nuclear power plants have been caused by human error or plumbing issues rather than advanced technology flaws. It also notes that such everyday events are not unlikely over time. Option D aligns with this by stating that continued operation makes serious accidents not improbable. \n\n**Answer: D**",
