mmit the crime is false.\" Wait, if we assume all assertions are true, then Wang didn't do it, but that leads to a contradiction because the perpetrator must be one of the three, but all three are ruled out. So the only way to resolve this is that at least one assertion is false. But does that mean specifically that Wang's evidence is false? Not necessarily. It could be that assertion 4 is false, meaning Zhu was actually at the crime scene. Or maybe assertion 1 is false. So I don't think we can specifically conclude that Wang's evidence is false; it's just that at least one is false. So option C might not be correct.\n\nOption D: \"Zhu Tong was definitely not in the audience at the football match.\" Assertion 4 says that the TV screen showed Zhu was there. If assertion 4 is true, then Zhu was definitely there. But if assertion 4 is false, then Zhu wasn't there. However, from the previous reasoning, we know that at least one assertion is false. So it's possible that assertion 4 is false, meaning Zhu was at the crime scene. But we can't definitively say that Zhu was definitely not there because assertion 4 could be true. So option D isn't necessarily correct.\n\nSo putting it all together, the correct answer should be option B because if all four assertions were true, it would lead to a contradiction, meaning at least one must be false.\n</think>\n\nThe correct answer is:\n\n**B) At least one of the above assertions is false.**\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nThe four assertions lead to a contradiction if all are assumed true. Specifically, if Chen Hu and Wang Guang are ruled out, and Zhu Tong is shown to be elsewhere, there's no perpetrator, contradicting assertion 2. Thus, at least one assertion must be false.",
