      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I need to figure out which of the options A, B, C, or D describes a virtual team based on the given definition. Let me start by understanding the definition provided.\n\nThe context says a virtual team is a community of two or more people from different regions and organizations. They use communication and information technology to connect. They have complementary skills or technologies, share common goals, evaluation methods, and work processes. They also jointly take responsibility for the final results.\n\nSo, the key points are:\n1. Two or more people.\n2. Across regions and organizations.\n3. Connected via communication and IT.\n4. Complementary skills or tech.\n5. Common goals, evaluation, work methods.\n6. Jointly responsible for results.\n\nNow, let's look at each option.\n\nOption A: A publishing firm contacts several experts to compile a textbook. They work in different units and coordinate via phone, fax, and network. So, they're from different units (maybe different organizations?), using IT, have a common goal (compile the textbook), and share responsibilities. This seems to fit.\n\nOption B: A company transfers employees from different departments to form a new product team. They're all from the same company, so same organization. The definition mentions across organizations, so this might not fit. Also, they're physically moved, so maybe not using IT as the main communication. Not sure, but probably not a virtual team.\n\nOption C: Xiao Li consults experts via internet and phone, establishing regular contacts. So, he's working with experts from different places, using IT. They have complementary skills (he's not familiar with the field, so they help him). They have a common goal (solving his technical problem), and he's responsible for the outcome. This seems to fit too.\n\nOption D: Three people chatting online and becoming friends. They have similar experiences and hobbies. This doesn't mention a common goal, complementary skills, or joint responsibility. It's more of a social group, not a team working towards a goal. So, probably not a virtual team.\n\nNow, between A and C. The definition says the team is composed of two or more people across regions and organizations. In A, the experts are from different units, which might imply different organizations. In C, Xiao Li is consulting experts, but it's unclear if they're part of a team or just individual consultations. The question is about which one belongs to a virtual team, so the team needs to be a group working together towards a common goal.\n\nOption A is a team working together on a textbook, using IT, from different units (possibly organizations). Option C is more about individual consultations rather than a team working together. So, A fits better.\n\nWait, but in C, Xiao Li is establishing regular contacts with experts, which might form a team. But the question is about the team, not the individual. So, the team in A is the group of experts compiling the textbook, which fits all the criteria. In C, it's Xiao Li and the experts, but it's not clear if they form a team or just individual help.\n\nSo, I think A is the correct answer.\n</think>\n\nThe correct answer is:\n\n**A) A publishing firm contacted several experts to jointly compile a new textbook. They work in different units and coordinating their work is mainly through telephone, fax, and network.**\n\n**Explanation:**\nOption A fits the definition of a virtual team as it involves individuals from different units (possibly different organizations) working together towards a common goal (compiling a textbook) using communication technology, with shared responsibilities.",
