ong values. The paragraph doesn't directly talk about values being wrong, just that people avoid conflicting views, which might lead to less critical thinking but not necessarily wrong values.\n\nOption D: People who like reading are calmer than those who like watching TV. The paragraph doesn't mention anything about people's personalities or calmness, so this seems unrelated.\n\nNow, between A and B. The paragraph does talk about how reading helps with thinking and how TV leads to less reflection. So A is about the effectiveness of reading for thinking. B is about how the method of receiving information affects behavior.\n\nThe paragraph seems to be making a point about the medium's impact on how people process information. It's not just about thinking but also about behavior, like changing channels or relying on intuition. So B might be a broader point.\n\nWait, but the first part specifically contrasts reading and watching TV in terms of information retention and reflection, which are cognitive processes. So maybe A is more directly supported.\n\nBut the paragraph also discusses how the method of reception (reading vs. watching) affects behavior, like avoiding conflicting views and relying on intuition. So B is also a valid point.\n\nHmm, I'm a bit torn. Let me think again. The paragraph starts by comparing reading and watching TV in terms of information retention and reflection, then moves on to how the medium affects behavior when faced with conflicting information. So it's talking about both the cognitive benefits of reading and the behavioral impacts of watching TV.\n\nBut the question is asking what the paragraph is intended to illustrate. The main focus seems to be on the difference in how information is processed based on the medium. So it's about the way information is received affecting how people think and behave.\n\nTherefore, option B seems to capture the broader idea that the method of receiving information (reading vs. watching TV) affects people's 