      "index": 0,
      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I've got this logical reasoning problem here, and I need to figure out the correct answer. Let me read through the context and the question again to make sure I understand everything.\n\nThe context says that when a major political scandal happens before an election, if voters blame all parties equally, almost all incumbents get re-elected, regardless of their party. But if voters blame only one party, then incumbents from that party are likely to lose to challengers from other parties. Also, the proportion of incumbents seeking reelection is high and doesn't change much from election to election.\n\nThe question is asking which principle best explains the contrast in voters' reactions. The options are A, B, C, D.\n\nLet me break this down. So, the key points are:\n\n1. When voters blame all parties equally, incumbents from all parties get re-elected.\n2. When voters blame only one party, incumbents from that party lose.\n3. The number of incumbents seeking reelection is high and constant.\n\nI need to find a principle that explains why voters react differently in these two scenarios.\n\nLooking at the options:\n\nOption A: Major scandals can be blamed on incumbents, but whether they should be voted out depends on their challengers. Hmm, the context doesn't mention anything about the challengers' quality or who they are. It's more about the blame being spread or concentrated. So maybe A isn't the best fit.\n\nOption B: When all parties are blamed, that judgment is more accurate than blaming only one party. The context doesn't talk about the accuracy of the blame; it's more about the outcome of the election. So B might not be the right principle here.\n\nOption C: When scandals are more the responsibility of parties than individual incumbents, the responsible party should be penalized when possible. That makes sense because if voters blame all parties, they don't penalize any specific party, so incumbents get re-elected. But if they blame one party, they penalize that party by defeating its incumbents. This seems to align with the context.\n\nOption D: Incumbents who are rightly blamed shouldn't seek reelection, but if they do, they shouldn't be returned. The context says that incumbents do seek reelection regardless, and whether they get re-elected depends on the blame distribution. So D doesn't quite fit because it's more about individual blame rather than party blame.\n\nSo, putting it together, the principle in C seems to explain the contrast. When the blame is spread across all parties, voters don't penalize any party, so incumbents are re-elected. When the blame is on one party, voters penalize that party by defeating its incumbents. That matches the described behavior.\n</think>\n\nThe correct answer is:\n\n**C) When major political scandals are less the responsibility of individual incumbents than of the parties to which they belong, whatever party was responsible must be penalized when possible.**\n\n**Explanation:**\nThe principle in option C aligns with the described voter behavior. When voters distribute blame equally among all parties, they don't penalize any specific party, leading to incumbents' reelection. Conversely, when blame is concentrated on one party, voters penalize that party by defeating its incumbents, explaining the contrast in reactions.",
