a long history of support, but Pedro's argument is more about the principle of donations not creating obligations, not specifically about the duration of support. So C might not capture the core disagreement.\n\nD) Whether loyalty should be a consideration. Nick brings up loyalty, but Pedro's argument is more about the nature of donations and obligations. While loyalty is part of Nick's reasoning, Pedro's counter is about the principle of donations, not directly about loyalty. So D might not be the main point.\n\nSo, the main disagreement is about whether accepting donations creates a special obligation. Therefore, the answer should be B.\n</think>\n\nThe dialogue highlights a disagreement between Nick and Pedro regarding whether accepting donations imposes a special obligation on the university. Nick believes loyalty and past support should influence the decision, while Pedro argues that donations shouldn't create such obligations. \n\n**Answer: B) The acceptance of donations places a university under a special obligation to the donor**",
