      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I need to figure out which of the options A, B, C, or D is a negative image according to the given definitions. Let me start by understanding the context provided.\n\nThe context talks about continuous contrast, which is about how the human eye perceives color contrast over time. It's divided into positive and negative images. A positive image is when the original object's color is temporarily retained after the object is no longer visible. On the other hand, a negative image is when the visual image is temporarily colored with the opposite color after the object is removed.\n\nSo, the key here is to identify which option describes a situation where, after looking at a color, the afterimage is the opposite color. Let's go through each option one by one.\n\nOption A: \"A continuous projection of a static screen at 24 frames per second creates a dynamic picture in front of your eyes.\" Hmm, this sounds more like how movies work, where a series of static images create the illusion of motion. It doesn't mention anything about afterimages or color contrast, so I don't think this is related to positive or negative images. So probably not A.\n\nOption B: \"After staring at a red object, even if you move it away, you will still feel red appear in front of you.\" This seems like a positive image because the original color (red) is retained after the object is removed. So B is positive, not negative.\n\nOption C: \"After staring at red for a long time, the vision moves quickly to white, not white, but green.\" Wait, that's a bit confusing. Let me parse it again. It says after staring at red, when you move your vision quickly to white, you don't see white but green. So, the afterimage is green instead of red. Since green is the complementary color of red, this would be a negative image. So C seems to fit the definition of a negative image.\n\nOption D: \"Red is paired with yellow, and red is sometimes seen as a color with purple, and sometimes with yellow as a color with green.\" This seems to be talking about color pairing and how colors can be perceived differently when paired with others. It doesn't directly describe an afterimage effect, so I don't think D is the answer.\n\nWait, let me double-check. The context says negative image is when the visual image is temporarily colored with the original object's opposite color after the stimulation stops. So in option C, after looking at red, when you look at white, you see green. Green is the opposite of red, so that's a negative image. Option B is positive because it's the same color. So the correct answer should be C.\n</think>\n\nThe correct answer is C. \n\n**Step-by-Step Explanation:**\n\n1. **Understanding Definitions:**\n   - **Positive Image:** Retention of the original color after the object is removed.\n   - **Negative Image:** Afterimage appears in the complementary (opposite) color.\n\n2. **Analyzing Options:**\n   - **A:** Describes motion illusion, unrelated to afterimages.\n   - **B:** Retains red, indicating a positive image.\n   - **C:** After red, seeing green (complementary) when looking at white, indicating a negative image.\n   - **D:** Discusses color pairing, not afterimages.\n\n3. **Conclusion:**\n   - Option C fits the definition of a negative image.\n\n**Answer:** C",
