      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I'm trying to solve this logical reasoning problem. Let me read through the context and question carefully.\n\nThe context says that the Earth takes about 365.25 days to orbit the Sun, which is why we have a leap year every four years. Normally, a year is 365 days, but every fourth year, we add an extra day, making it 366 days. The year is divided into 52 weeks, each with 7 days, which totals 364 days. That leaves 1 or 2 days unaccounted for each year, causing anniversaries to shift days of the week each year.\n\nThe proposal suggests that the last day of each year (December 31) and the extra day in a leap year (February 29) should not belong to any week. This way, January 1 would always be a Sunday, which would make the calendar consistent every year.\n\nNow, the question is asking which group would most likely experience continued scheduling conflicts if this proposal is implemented. The options are A, B, C, D.\n\nLet me break down each option:\n\nOption A: Employed people with strict religious observances requiring them to refrain from working every seventh day. If the calendar is fixed with January 1 as Sunday, then every seventh day would be consistent. So, their observances would align with the same days each year, possibly reducing conflicts. So maybe this group wouldn't have continued issues.\n\nOption B: People with birthdays or anniversaries on December 30 or 31. The proposal says December 31 would not belong to any week. So, if someone's birthday is on December 30, that's still part of a week, but December 31 would be outside. So, their anniversaries might not fall on the same day of the week each year, but since the last day is excluded, maybe their scheduling could be affected. But I'm not sure yet.\n\nOption C: People who plan events several years in advance. If the calendar is fixed, planning events years ahead would be easier because each date would fall on the same day of the week every year. So this group might actually benefit, not have conflicts.\n\nOption D: Employed people with three-day breaks when holidays are on Mondays or Fridays. If the calendar is fixed, holidays would always fall on the same day each year. So, if a holiday is on a Monday, it would always be a Monday, making the three-day break consistent. This might reduce conflicts, not cause them.\n\nWait, but the question is about continued scheduling conflicts. So which group would still have issues despite the proposal?\n\nLooking back, the proposal makes January 1 always Sunday, so each date would fall on the same weekday every year. That would help most people, but maybe some groups would still have issues.\n\nOption B: If December 31 is excluded, then December 30 would be the last day of the year. So, someone with a birthday on December 31 would have their birthday fall on a non-week day, which might cause issues. But the proposal says that the last day (December 31) and the leap day (February 29) are outside weeks. So, December 31 would not be part of any week, meaning it's like an extra day. So, if someone's birthday is on December 31, it would not fall on a specific weekday each year because it's outside the weekly cycle. Similarly, December 30 would still be part of the week, so its weekday would be consistent.\n\nWait, but the proposal says that the last day of each year (December 31) and the extra day every fourth year (February 29) are outside weeks. So, each year, December 31 is not part of any week, meaning it's like an extra day. So, the next year, January 1 is always Sunday. So, December 31 would not affect the weekly cycle because it's excluded.\n\nSo, for someone with a birthday on December 31, their birthday would not fall on a specific weekday each year because it's outside the weekly structure. But since the proposal makes the rest of the year consistent, maybe their birthday would still cause issues because it's not part of the weekly schedule. So, they might have scheduling conflicts because their birthday is on an extra day not part of any week.\n\nAlternatively, if the last day is excluded, then the year effectively ends on December 30, and December 31 is a separate day. So, people with birthdays on December 31 would have their birthday on a day that's not part of any week, which might cause issues in scheduling, like not knowing which day of the week it falls on each year.\n\nWait, but the proposal says that the last day (December 31) and the leap day (February 29) are outside weeks. So, each year, December 31 is not part of any week, meaning it's like an extra day. So, the next year, January 1 is always Sunday. So, December 31 would not affect the weekly cycle because it's excluded.\n\nSo, for someone with a birthday on December 31, their birthday would not fall on a specific weekday each year because it's outside the weekly structure. But since the proposal makes the rest of the year consistent, maybe their birthday would still cause issues because it's not part of the weekly schedule. So, they might have scheduling conflicts because their birthday is on an extra day not part of any week.\n\nAlternatively, if the last day is excluded, then the year effectively ends on December 30, and December 31 is a separate day. So, people with birthdays on December 31 would have their birthday on a day that's not part of any week, which might cause issues in scheduling, like not knowing which day of the week it falls on each year.\n\nWait, but the proposal makes January 1 always Sunday, so the rest of the year's dates would fall on the same weekdays each year. So, December 30 would be a specific weekday each year, but December 31 would be outside. So, if someone's birthday is on December 31, it's not part of any week, so their birthday would not align with the same weekday each year, causing scheduling issues.\n\nSo, the group in option B would have continued conflicts because their anniversaries are on days that are outside the weekly structure, making their