      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I've got this logical reasoning problem here, and I need to figure out the correct answer. Let me try to break it down step by step.\n\nFirst, the context gives me three judgments that are all true. I'll list them out to make it clearer:\n\n1. Either A is on a business trip, or B is on a business trip, or C is on a business trip. So, at least one of them must be on a trip.\n2. If A is on a business trip, then C is also on a business trip. So, A implies C.\n3. C can't leave because of his busy work. Hmm, this seems like C is not on a business trip. So, C is not going.\n\nNow, the question is asking which option can be derived from these statements. The options are A, B, C, or D.\n\nLet me start by analyzing each statement.\n\nFrom statement 3, it's clear that C is not on a business trip. So, C is out of the picture. That means, according to statement 1, either A or B must be on a trip because at least one of A, B, or C has to be. Since C isn't, it's either A or B or both.\n\nNow, looking at statement 2: If A is on a trip, then C must also be on a trip. But wait, from statement 3, we know C isn't on a trip. So, if A were on a trip, that would mean C is also on a trip, which contradicts statement 3. Therefore, A cannot be on a trip because that would make C have to be on a trip, which isn't possible.\n\nSo, if A isn't on a trip, then from statement 1, B must be on a trip because at least one of A, B, or C has to be. Since C isn't, and A isn't, B must be.\n\nSo, putting it all together: A is not on a trip, B is on a trip, and C is not.\n\nNow, looking at the options:\n\nA) A is on business while B is not. But we just concluded B is on a trip, so this can't be right.\n\nB) B is on business while A is not. That fits with our conclusion.\n\nC) Both A and B are on business. But we know A isn't, so this is wrong.\n\nD) Neither A nor B is on business. But we know B is, so this is also wrong.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer should be option B.\n</think>\n\nThe correct answer is B) B is on business while A is not.\n\n**Step-by-Step Explanation:**\n\n1. **Statement 3** tells us that C is not on a business trip.\n2. **Statement 2** says if A is on a trip, then C must also be. Since C isn't, A can't be on a trip either.\n3. **Statement 1** requires at least one of A, B, or C to be on a trip. Since A and C aren't, B must be.\n\n**Answer:** B) B is on business while A is not.",
