 the Arctic, the normal temperature is much lower, but the microbes are still active because they're adapted to that environment.\n\nWait, but Charlene didn't specify that \"normal\" is the region's normal temperature. She just said \"a region's temperature drops below normal.\" So, maybe Olaf took \"normal\" as a universal temperature, like a standard, whereas Charlene meant the region's own normal temperature. So, if a region's temperature drops below its own normal, then the microbes become less active. But in the Arctic, their normal is already cold, so even if it drops a bit, maybe they're still active. Or perhaps the study showed that regardless of the region's normal, the activity was the same.\n\nAlternatively, maybe Olaf misinterpreted \"normal\" as a specific temperature threshold, like a global standard, whereas Charlene meant each region's own normal. So, when Charlene said \"drops below normal,\" she meant each region's own normal, but Olaf thought it was a fixed temperature, leading him to counter with the study showing similar activity in both regions.\n\nSo, the term that Olaf misinterpreted is \"normal.\" He didn't consider that \"normal\" could refer to each region's specific temperature, thinking instead that it's a universal standard. Therefore, the correct answer is D) Normal.\n</think>\n\nOlaf misinterpreted Charlene's use of the term \"normal,\" assuming it referred to a universal temperature rather than each region's specific normal. \n\nAnswer: D) Normal",
