      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I've got this logical reasoning problem here, and I need to figure out the correct answer. Let me start by reading the context and the question carefully.\n\nThe context is a statement: \"If you close all the wrong doors, truth will be shut out.\" Hmm, that sounds a bit abstract, but I think I can break it down. It's a conditional statement, right? So in logical terms, it's like saying \"If P, then Q,\" where P is \"closing all the wrong doors\" and Q is \"truth being shut out.\"\n\nNow, the question is asking which of the options is closest in meaning to this statement. The options are A, B, C, and D. I need to analyze each one to see which matches the original statement's meaning.\n\nLet me recall some logic basics. A conditional statement \"If P, then Q\" can be represented as P \u2192 Q. The contrapositive of this is \"If not Q, then not P,\" which is logically equivalent. The converse would be \"If Q, then P,\" and the inverse is \"If not P, then not Q.\" These are not equivalent to the original statement.\n\nSo, the original statement is P \u2192 Q. Let's look at each option:\n\nOption A: \"If truth is shut out, it must be that you close all the wrong doors.\" That sounds like the converse of the original statement. The original was P \u2192 Q, and this is Q \u2192 P. So A is the converse, which isn't necessarily true just because the original is true.\n\nOption B: \"Truth cannot be shut out unless all the doors of wrong are closed.\" Let me parse this. \"Cannot be shut out unless...\" translates to \"If truth is shut out, then all wrong doors are closed.\" Wait, that's similar to the converse again. Because \"unless\" often implies a necessary condition. So it's saying that for truth to be shut out, all wrong doors must be closed. That's Q \u2192 P, which is the converse again. So B is also the converse.\n\nOption C: \"If the truth is not shut out, then the door to all wrong must not be closed.\" Let's see. \"Truth is not shut out\" is \u00acQ, and \"door to all wrong must not be closed\" is \u00acP. So this is \u00acQ \u2192 \u00acP, which is the inverse of the original statement. The inverse isn't equivalent to the original, so C is not correct.\n\nOption D: \"To accept the truth, we must reject wrong.\" Hmm, this is a bit more abstract. Let me think. \"Accept the truth\" could mean that truth is not shut out, so \u00acQ. \"Reject wrong\" might mean not closing the wrong doors, so \u00acP. So this is saying that to have \u00acQ, you must have \u00acP. That's \u00acQ \u2192 \u00acP, which is the inverse again. Wait, but earlier I thought the inverse was C. So D is also the inverse? Or maybe I'm misinterpreting.\n\nWait, maybe I'm overcomplicating. Let me re-express the original statement: P \u2192 Q. The contrapositive is \u00acQ \u2192 \u00acP. So the contrapositive is logically equivalent. Now, looking at the options, which one is the contrapositive?\n\nOption C is \u00acQ \u2192 \u00acP, which is the contrapositive. Wait, no, I thought C was the inverse. Wait, no, the inverse is \u00acP \u2192 \u00acQ, which is not the same as the contrapositive. The contrapositive is \u00acQ \u2192 \u00acP, which is actually the same as the inverse of the converse. So, in this case, the contrapositive would be \"If truth is not shut out, then not all wrong doors are closed.\" Which is exactly what option C says. So C is the contrapositive, which is equivalent to the original statement.\n\nWait, but earlier I thought C was the inverse. Let me clarify. The original is P \u2192 Q. The contrapositive is \u00acQ \u2192 \u00acP. The inverse is \u00acP \u2192 \u00acQ, which is not equivalent. So C is the contrapositive, which is equivalent, so C should be correct.\n\nBut wait, let me check the options again. Option C says, \"If the truth is not shut out, then the door to all wrong must not be closed.\" That's \u00acQ \u2192 \u00acP, which is the contrapositive, so it's equivalent. So C is correct.\n\nBut w