      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I have this logical reasoning problem to solve. Let me try to understand it step by step. The context talks about two concepts: division and decomposition. Division is a method where you take a broader concept and split it into smaller, more specific categories. These smaller categories are species concepts, and they should all be mutually exclusive and collectively exhaustive. On the other hand, decomposition is breaking down an object into parts, but the parts don't have a species relationship with the whole. So, decomposition isn't about categorizing but rather about parts and wholes.\n\nThe question is asking which of the options correctly applies division as defined. So, I need to look at each option and see if it's an example of division or decomposition.\n\nLet's go through each option one by one.\n\nOption A: \"The definition is divided into defined items, defined items and defined joint items.\" Hmm, this seems a bit confusing. It mentions \"defined items\" twice, which might be a typo. Maybe it's supposed to be different categories. But regardless, the main point is whether this is a division. Definitions can be broken down into parts, but I'm not sure if this is a proper division. It might be more about decomposition since it's breaking down a definition into its components, which are parts rather than species.\n\nOption B: \"Hohhot is divided into xincheng district, huimin district, saihan district and yuquan district.\" Hohhot is a city, and these are its districts. So, this is breaking down a whole (the city) into its parts (the districts). That sounds like decomposition because it's about parts of a whole, not categorizing a broader concept into species.\n\nOption C: \"Celestial bodies in the universe can be divided into natural and man-made celestial bodies.\" Celestial bodies are a broad category. Dividing them into natural (like planets, stars) and man-made (like satellites, space stations) seems like a proper divis