nishment and deprivation of rights. So, they can coexist, which might mean it's not a proper division because division requires that each member of the genus belongs to exactly one species.\n\nTherefore, option C is a proper division, while option D is not because the categories can overlap. So the correct answer is C.\n</think>\n\nThe correct answer is:\n\n**C) Celestial bodies in the universe can be divided into natural and man-made celestial bodies.**\n\n**Explanation:**\nDivision involves splitting a broader concept into mutually exclusive and exhaustive categories. Option C correctly divides celestial bodies into natural (e.g., planets, stars) and man-made (e.g., satellites), which are distinct and cover all possibilities. Option D, while categorizing penalties, may involve overlapping types, making it less suitable as a clear division.",
