Whether the new process should be adopted. The Supervisor agrees with the conclusion that it's not profitable, so he's not disagreeing on adoption.\n\nC) Whether the new process is more expensive to run. The Plant Manager said it's more expensive because of reheating. The Supervisor says the furnaces are fuel-efficient, which might mean the running cost isn't higher, so he's disagreeing on point C.\n\nD) Whether closed furnaces are more fuel-efficient. The Plant Manager didn't make a statement about fuel efficiency; he just said the process is more expensive to run. The Supervisor is introducing the point that the furnaces are fuel-efficient, so he's disagreeing with the Plant Manager's assumption about the cost, not directly about fuel efficiency.\n\nWait, but the question is about the point the Supervisor disagrees with. The Plant Manager didn't say anything about fuel efficiency; he just said the process is more expensive to run. The Supervisor is pointing out that the furnaces are fuel-efficient, which might mean that the running cost isn't as high as the Plant Manager thought. So the Supervisor is disagreeing with the Plant Manager's point that the new process is more expensive to run because of fuel efficiency.\n\nSo the point of disagreement is whether the new process is more expensive to run. Therefore, the answer is C.\n\nWait, but option D is about whether closed furnaces are more fuel-efficient. The Plant Manager didn't make a statement about that. He just said the process is more expensive to run. The Supervisor is introducing the fuel efficiency as a counterpoint, so he's disagreeing with the Plant Manager's reasoning about the cost, not directly about fuel efficiency. So the point of disagreement is about the cost, which is option C.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the Plant Manager didn't mention fuel efficiency, so the Supervisor is introducing a new point, which is that the furnaces are fuel-efficient, which would make the running cost not as high. So the Supervisor is disagreeing with the Plant Manager's assertion that the process is more expensive to run because the furnaces are fuel-efficient. So the point is about the cost, which is option C.\n\nWait, but the options are about what the Supervisor disagrees with. The Plant Manager said the process is more expensive to run because of reheating. The Supervisor says the furnaces are fuel-efficient, which might mean that the running cost isn't higher. So the Supervisor is disagreeing with the Plant Manager's point that the process is more expensive to run. Therefore, the answer is C.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the point is about whether the furnaces are fuel-efficient, but the Plant Manager didn't make a statement about that. So the Supervisor is introducing a new point, which is that the furnaces are fuel-efficient, which would affect the running cost. So the point of disagreement is about the running cost, which is option C.\n\nI think the correct answer is C.\n</think>\n\nThe supervisor disagrees with the plant manager's assertion that the new process is more expensive to run, arguing that the latest closed furnaces are fuel-efficient, which would mitigate the increased running costs.\n\n**Answer: C**",
