      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I've got this logical reasoning problem here, and I need to figure out which option weakens the argument the most. Let me try to break it down step by step.\n\nFirst, let me read the context carefully. The government of Sperland wants to reduce waste of raw materials by requiring household appliances to be broken down for salvage when discarded. To cover the cost of salvage, they're planning to charge a fee. Now, the fee is going to be imposed when the appliance is first sold. But the argument presented is that imposing the fee at the time of salvage would be more effective because consumers tend to keep old appliances longer if they have to pay a fee for discarding them. So, the idea is that if you make people pay when they discard, they'll hold onto their old appliances longer, which reduces waste.\n\nThe question is asking which option, if true, would most seriously weaken this argument. So, I need to find the option that undermines the reasoning behind why imposing the fee at salvage is better.\n\nLet me look at the options one by one.\n\nOption A: Many nonfunctioning appliances that are currently discarded could be repaired at relatively little expense. Hmm, so if appliances can be easily repaired, maybe people would repair them instead of discarding them. That could reduce waste, but does it weaken the argument? The argument is about the fee's timing affecting how long people keep appliances. If appliances can be repaired, maybe the fee's timing isn't as crucial because people would just fix them. But I'm not sure if this directly weakens the argument about the fee's effectiveness. It might be more of a side point.\n\nOption B: The fee provides manufacturers with no incentive to produce appliances that are more durable. So, if the fee doesn't encourage manufacturers to make better, longer-lasting appliances, then the fee's impact on reducing waste might be limited. But the argument is about the timing of the fee, not about the manufacturers' incentives. So this might not directly weaken the argument about the fee's timing. It's more about the fee's overall effectiveness, not the timing aspect.\n\nOption C: Increasing the cost of disposing of an appliance properly increases the incentive to dispose of it improperly. Oh, this seems relevant. If people have to pay a fee when they discard, they might be more tempted to dispose of appliances improperly, like throwing them in the trash instead of salvaging them. That would mean the fee doesn't achieve the intended effect because people might avoid paying it by not salvaging. So this directly weakens the argument because it suggests that imposing the fee at salvage might not reduce waste as intended\u2014it could lead to more improper disposal.\n\nOption D: For people who have bought new appliances recently, the salvage fee would not need to be paid for a number of years. Wait, if the fee is imposed at the time of sale, then when they buy a new appliance, they pay the fee upfront. But if they don't have to pay it for a few years, that might mean they don't feel the impact immediately. However, the argument is about imposing the fee at salvage time. If the fee is paid upfront, then the timing is different. But this option is talking about when the fee is paid, not about the effect on disposal behavior. So I'm not sure if this weakens the argument about the effectiveness of the fee timing.\n\nWait, let me think again. The argument is that imposing the fee at salvage time would make people keep their appliances longer because they don't want to pay the fee when discarding. So if the fee is imposed at the time of sale, people pay it upfront, and then when they discard, they don't have to pay again. So the argument is that paying at salvage time is better because it incentivizes keeping the appliance longer.\n\nOption C says that if the cost of proper disposal increases, people might dispose improperly. So if the fee is high, people might just throw the appliance in the trash instead of paying to salvage it. That would mean the fee doesn't help reduce waste because the appliances aren't being salvaged; they're being improperly disposed of. So this directly undermines the argument that imposing the fee at salvage time would reduce waste.\n\nOption A is about repair, which is a different way to reduce waste, but it doesn't directly weaken the argument about fee timing. Option B is about manufacturer incentives, which is a separate issue. Option D is about when the fee is paid, but it doesn't directly address whether the fee at salvage time would reduce waste.\n\nSo, I think the correct answer is C because it shows that imposing the fee at salvage time might lead to more improper disposal, thus not reducing waste as intended.\n</think>\n\nThe correct answer is:\n\n**C) Increasing the cost of disposing of an appliance properly increases the incentive to dispose of it improperly.**\n\n**Explanation:**\nThe argument posits that imposing a fee at the time of salvage would encourage consumers to keep their appliances longer, thereby reducing waste. However, option C weakens this argument by suggesting that a higher disposal fee could lead to more improper disposal methods, such as illegal dumping, which would not reduce waste as intended. This directly undermines the effectiveness of the fee in achieving the government's goal.",
