      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I need to figure out what this paragraph is trying to illustrate. Let me read through it again carefully.\n\nThe context talks about Wittgenstein being a student of Moore at Cambridge. Russell, another philosopher, asks Moore who his best student is, and Moore says Wittgenstein. The reason Moore gives is that Wittgenstein always had a blank look and endless questions. Later, Wittgenstein becomes more famous than Russell. Someone asks why Russell fell behind, and Wittgenstein responds that Russell had no problem.\n\nHmm, so the key points here are that Wittgenstein was always questioning and had endless questions, which made him stand out as Moore's best student. On the other hand, Russell didn't have any problems, which led to him falling behind.\n\nLooking at the options:\n\nA) Doubting and questioning are always the secret of success. Well, the paragraph does mention that Wittgenstein had endless questions, which contributed to his success. But does it say that doubting is always the secret? Maybe, but I'm not sure if it's the best fit.\n\nB) Curiosity is the internal driving force of research. Curiosity would relate to asking questions, which Wittgenstein did. This seems relevant, but I'm not sure if it's the main point.\n\nC) Appreciation is an important factor in success. The paragraph doesn't mention anything about appreciation, so I don't think this is the right answer.\n\nD) Finding problems is the premise of continuous progress. Wittgenstein had endless questions, which implies he was always finding problems to solve. Russell didn't have any problems, so he didn't progress as much. This seems to fit well because the paragraph emphasizes that having problems (or questions) leads to progress, while not having them leads to stagnation.\n\nSo, putting it all together, the paragraph is illustrating that finding problems is essential for continuous progress. Therefore, the correct answer should be D.\n</think>\n\nThe paragraph emphasizes that Wittgenstein's success was due to his endless questioning, which led to his fame surpassing Russell's. Russell's lack of problems resulted in his stagnation. This highlights the importance of identifying issues for progress.\n\n**Answer: D) Finding problems is the premise of continuous progress**",
