      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I've got this logical reasoning problem here, and I need to figure out the correct answer. Let me try to break it down step by step.\n\nFirst, the context is about a university administrator explaining the criteria for funding a new department. The administrator says that any proposal for a new department won't be funded if two conditions are met: one, there are fewer than 50 people per year available for hire in that field, and two, the proposed department duplicates more than 25% of the material covered in an existing department. \n\nBut then, the administrator mentions that the proposed Area Studies Department does duplicate more than 25% of the material in the existing Anthropology Department. Despite this duplication, they say they will fund the new department. So, that's interesting because according to the initial rule, if both conditions are met, it shouldn't be funded. But here, even though duplication is over 25%, they're funding it. That must mean that the other condition isn't met. \n\nSo, the two conditions are:\n1. Fewer than 50 people available per year.\n2. Duplicates more than 25% of an existing department.\n\nIf both are true, the proposal isn't funded. But since the proposal is being funded despite the duplication, it must mean that the first condition isn't met. In other words, there are at least 50 people available per year in the field of Area Studies. Because if there were fewer than 50, then both conditions would be met, and funding would be denied. But since funding is happening, the first condition must not be true. So, the number of people available is 50 or more.\n\nNow, looking at the options:\n\nOption A: If the proposed department didn't duplicate more than 25%, then it wouldn't be funded. Wait, that doesn't make sense. The rule says if both conditions are met, it's not funded. If duplication is less than or equal to 25%, then even if the first condition is met (fewer than 50 people), the duplication condition isn't met, so the department might still be funded. So A seems incorrect because it's suggesting that without duplication, funding is denied, which isn't necessarily the case.\n\nOption B: The proposed department doesn't duplicate more than 25% in any other department besides Anthropology. The administrator only mentions duplication with Anthropology. There's no information about other departments, so we can't conclude anything about them. So B might not be correct.\n\nOption C: The field has fewer than 50 people available. But we just reasoned that since the department is being funded despite duplication, the number must be at least 50. So C is the opposite of what we concluded.\n\nOption D: The field has at least 50 people available. That aligns with our earlier reasoning. Since duplication is over 25%, but funding is happening, the other condition (fewer than 50) must not be true. So D is correct.\n\nWait, let me double-check. The rule is: if (fewer than 50 AND duplicates >25%), then not funded. The contrapositive would be: if funded, then either not fewer than 50 OR not duplicates >25%. In this case, the department is funded, and we know duplicates >25%, so the other part must be true: not fewer than 50, meaning at least 50. So yes, D is correct.\n</think>\n\nThe correct answer is D. The field of area studies has at least 50 people per year available for hire.\n\n**Step-by-Step Explanation:**\n\n1. **Understand the Funding Criteria:**\n   - A new department is not funded if:\n     - Fewer than 50 people are available per year in the field.\n     - The department duplicates more than 25% of an existing department's material.\n\n2. **Analyze the Given Information:**\n   - The Area Studies Department duplicates more than 25% of the Anthropology Department's material.\n   - Despite this duplication, the department is being funded.\n\n3. **Apply Logical Reasoning:**\n   - Since the department is funded, at least one of the two conditions must not be met.\n   - The duplication condition is met (over 25%), so the other condition (fewer than 50 people) must not be met.\n   - Therefore, there are at least 50 people available per year in the field of Area Studies.\n\n4. **Evaluate the Options:**\n   - **A:** Incorrect. The absence of duplication doesn't necessarily mean funding is denied.\n   - **B:** Incorrect. There's no information about other departments.\n   - **C:** Incorrect. The field has enough people to meet the funding criteria.\n   - **D:** Correct. The field meets the 50-person requirement.\n\n**Answer:** D) The field of area studies has at least 50 people per year available for hire.",
