      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I've got this logical reasoning problem here, and I need to figure out the correct answer. Let me start by reading through the context and the question carefully.\n\nThe context talks about some grass-roots units where the masses often talk back to cadres, and sometimes this leads to emotional antagonism between them. The main reason given is that some grass-roots cadres ignore the defensive psychology of the masses. Then it goes into what psychologists believe about defense psychology: when people are criticized, they instinctively develop a sense of defense. Even if they've made obvious mistakes, they might come up with objective reasons to justify themselves and dilute the negative effects. It also mentions that defense psychology has two main manifestations: explaining the problem through defense and evading responsibility.\n\nThe question is asking which of the options A, B, C, or D is correct based on the text.\n\nLet me look at each option one by one.\n\nOption A: \"The reason why the masses defend themselves is to let the cadres find out the real situation.\" Hmm, the context says that when criticized, people develop a defense psychology to justify themselves and dilute the adverse effects. It doesn't mention anything about wanting cadres to find the real situation. So I don't think A is correct.\n\nOption B: \"Grass-roots cadres should distinguish different situations and correctly deal with their defense towards the masses.\" Wait, the context says that cadres ignore the defensive psychology of the masses. So maybe cadres should be aware of this and handle it properly. But the option says \"their defense towards the masses.\" Wait, does it mean the cadres' defense towards the masses? Or the masses' defense towards the cadres? The context is about the masses defending themselves against cadres. So if cadres are ignoring the masses' defense, maybe they should handle it correctly. So B might be correct, but I'm not sure yet.\n