freedom of speech, there are many restrictions, so it's not absolute.\n\nOption D: Someone coined the famous saying of freedom of speech in the name of voltaire. The text does mention that the quote was falsely attributed to Voltaire, but the main emphasis seems to be more on the limitations of freedom of speech rather than the origin of the quote.\n\nSo, putting it all together, the text is pointing out that the West's promotion of freedom of speech is based on a false quote and that in reality, there are significant restrictions. Therefore, the main emphasis is that there's no absolute freedom of speech in the West, which is option C.\n</think>\n\nThe main emphasis of the text is that there is no absolute freedom of speech in the West, as evidenced by the restrictions and limitations discussed. \n\nAnswer: C) There is no absolute freedom of speech in the west.",
