      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I need to figure out which option correctly identifies the flaw in the researcher's argument. Let me start by understanding the argument presented.\n\nThe researcher says that people in opinion surveys often give answers they think the surveyor expects. This can make the survey results not reflect their true views. However, the researcher argues that in well-constructed surveys, the questions are worded in a way that doesn't indicate what answers the surveyor expects. Therefore, if a survey is well-constructed, the respondents' desire to meet the surveyor's expectations won't affect the results.\n\nHmm, so the researcher is assuming that if the survey doesn't indicate expectations, then respondents won't adjust their answers based on those expectations. But is that necessarily true?\n\nLet me look at the options one by one.\n\nOption A: An opinion survey that disguises the surveyor's expectations may be flawed in other ways unrelated to expectations. The researcher's argument is about the effect of expectations, so this option is pointing out other flaws, but the question is about what the argument overlooks regarding expectations. So maybe not the best fit.\n\nOption B: Some people might try to thwart the surveyor's expectations if they know what they expect. The researcher's argument is about the effect of respondents trying to meet expectations, not thwarting them. So this seems like a different issue, not directly related to the argument's oversight.\n\nOption C: Surveyors might have no expectations. The researcher's point is that even if surveyors have expectations, well-constructed surveys prevent respondents from knowing them. So this option is suggesting that surveyors might not have expectations, which doesn't directly address the argument's assumption about the effect of respondents' beliefs.\n\nOption D: Respondents' answers can be influenced by their beliefs about the surveyor's expectations, even if those beliefs are unfounded. Oh, wait, the researcher assumes that if the survey doesn't indicate expectations, then respondents won't adjust their answers. But what if respondents have their own beliefs about what the surveyor expects, regardless of how the questions are worded? Even if the survey is well-constructed, if respondents think the surveyor expects a certain answer, they might still adjust their response. So the researcher's argument overlooks this possibility because it assumes that without indication, respondents won't have such beliefs, but in reality, they might still have them based on other factors.\n\nSo the correct answer should be D because it points out that even without the survey indicating expectations, respondents might still base their answers on their own (possibly incorrect) beliefs about what the surveyor expects.\n</think>\n\nThe researcher's argument assumes that well-constructed surveys, which do not indicate expected answers, will prevent respondents from adjusting their answers based on perceived expectations. However, this overlooks the possibility that respondents might still base their answers on their own beliefs about the surveyor's expectations, even if those beliefs are unfounded. Therefore, the correct answer is:\n\n**D) The answers of opinion-survey respondents can be influenced by beliefs about the surveyor's expectations even if those beliefs are unfounded.**\n\nAnswer: D",
