hy the laws didn't lead to a decline. Wait, no, because if most victims weren't wearing seat belts, it suggests that the laws weren't effective in getting people to wear them, which is why the death rate didn't go down.\n\nWait, maybe I'm wrong. Let me think again.\n\nThe discrepancy is: laws passed, but no decline in deaths. Possible explanations:\n\nA: Laws not enforced, so people don't wear seat belts. So no decline. Helps.\n\nB: Speed limits increased, leading to more deaths. So even with seat belts, more deaths. Helps.\n\nC: Most killed weren't wearing seat belts. So seat belts are effective, but since people aren't wearing them, no decline. Helps.\n\nD: More traffic, more accidents, so no decline. Helps.\n\nWait, so all options help. But the question says which one does NOT help. So perhaps I'm missing something.\n\nWait, maybe option C doesn't help because it's just a result, not a cause. Like, the fact that most killed weren't wearing seat belts is a consequence of the laws not being followed, but it doesn't explain why the laws didn't lead to a decline. Or maybe it does, because it shows that seat belts are effective, but people aren't using them.\n\nWait, I'm getting confused. Let me try to think differently.\n\nThe safety report says that where strict laws are passed, fatalities decline. So the assumption is that the laws lead to more seat belt use, which reduces deaths.\n\nIn the city, the laws are in place, but no decline. So possible reasons:\n\n1. The laws aren't enforced, so people don't wear seat belts (A). This would mean the laws didn't lead to more seat belt use, so no decline.\n\n2. Something else is causing more deaths, like higher speeds (B), so even with seat belts, more deaths.\n\n3. More traffic (D), so more accidents, offsetting the decline.\n\n4. Most killed weren't wearing seat belts (C), which suggests that seat belts are effective, but since people aren't wearing them, the death rate didn't go down.\n\nWait, but if most killed weren't 