redistribute resources via taxation. Edward's argument is that governments have the right to redistribute because they allow emigration. So Edward would agree with B. What about Raphaela? She says that no government has the right to redistribute, regardless of emigration. So she would disagree with B. Therefore, they disagree on B.\n\nOption C: Any government that redistributes resources via taxation forces people to help others. Raphaela says that redistributing via taxation is forcing people to help, which is wrong. So she would agree with C. Edward, however, doesn't necessarily say that redistribution forces people to help. He just says that if a government allows emigration, then it's okay to redistribute. So Edward might not agree that redistribution inherently forces people to help. Therefore, they might disagree on C. Wait, but Edward doesn't explicitly say that redistribution doesn't force people; he just says that if emigration is allowed, then it's okay. So maybe Edward doesn't necessarily agree or disagree with C, but Raphaela does agree with C. So perhaps they don't necessarily disagree on C because Edward's stance isn't clear on whether redistribution forces people to help.\n\nOption D: Every government should allow people to help others voluntarily. Raphaela says that people can help others voluntarily, so she would agree with D. Edward doesn't directly address this, but his point is about allowing emigration, not necessarily about voluntary helping. So maybe Edward doesn't have a stance on D, or perhaps he agrees. So they might not disagree on D.\n\nWait, but let me think again. The question is about which statement they disagree on. So, looking back, Raphaela says that no government has the right to redistribute, and Edward says that they do, provided emigration is allowed. So the core disagreement is about whether governments have the right to redistribute. Now, looking at the options, which one reflects this disagreement.\n\nOption B says that any government that permits emigration has the right to redistribute. Edward would agree with this, while Raphaela would disagree because she says no government has the right, regardless of emigration. So they disagree on B.\n\nOption C says that any government that redistributes forces people to help. Raphaela would agree because she says redistributing is forcing. Edward doesn't necessarily agree or disagree with this statement because his point is about the right to redistribute given emigration, not whether redistribution forces helping. So perhaps they don't disagree on C.\n\nSo the main disagreement is on whether a government that allows emigration can redistribute, which is option B. Therefore, the answer should be B.\n</think>\n\nThe correct answer is:\n\n**B) Any government that permits emigration has the right to redistribute resources via taxation.**\n\n**Explanation:**\nRaphaela argues that no government should redistribute resources via taxation because it forces people to help others, which she deems morally wrong. Edward, however, believes that governments do have the right to redistribute resources as long as they allow people to emigrate, thus giving individuals the freedom to leave if they disagree with the government's policies. Their disagreement centers on whether a government that permits emigration has the right to redistribute resources, making option B the point of contention.",
