      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I need to figure out which principle justifies the nylon industry spokesperson's reasoning. Let me start by understanding the context.\n\nThe spokesperson is arguing that nylon is natural because its main components come from natural sources. They mention that nylon's main components are from petroleum and nitrogen in the atmosphere. They point out that the atmosphere is natural and petroleum comes from oil, which is from ancient plants, so that's natural too.\n\nSo the spokesperson is trying to counter the idea that nylon isn't natural. Their argument hinges on the origins of nylon's components being natural. Now, the question is asking which principle supports this reasoning.\n\nLooking at the options:\n\nOption A: A substance is natural if the origins of its main components are natural. That seems to fit because the spokesperson is saying nylon is natural because its components come from natural sources.\n\nOption B: A substance is unnatural only if the function it serves is unnatural. That doesn't seem relevant. The spokesperson isn't talking about the function of nylon, just its components.\n\nOption C: A substance is no more natural than its least natural component. Hmm, this is about the weakest link. If a substance has any unnatural component, it's less natural. But the spokesperson is saying nylon is natural because all components are natural, so this might not directly support their argument. It could be a counter-argument if someone says nylon has an unnatural component, but the spokesperson isn't addressing that.\n\nOption D: A substance is no less natural than the processes used in its production. The spokesperson didn't mention the production processes, only the origins of the components. So this might not be the right fit.\n\nSo the key point is that the spokesperson is focusing on the natural origins of nylon's components. Therefore, the principle that a substance is natural if its main components come from natural origins (Option A) would justify their reasoning.\n\nWait, but let me think again. Option C says a substance is no more natural than its least natural component. So if all components are natural, then the substance is natural. That could also support the argument. But the spokesperson's reasoning is more about the components being natural, not about the least component. So Option A directly states that if the origins are natural, the substance is natural, which aligns with the spokesperson's argument.\n\nAlternatively, Option C is about the substance not being more natural than its least component, which implies that if all components are natural, the substance is natural. So both A and C could be relevant. But the spokesperson is making a positive case that nylon is natural because its components are natural, which is more in line with Option A. Option C is more about not being less natural, which is a bit different.\n\nWait, no. Option C is saying that the substance's naturalness is determined by its least natural component. So if all components are natural, then the substance is natural. So it's a way of saying that as long as all components are natural, the substance is natural. So both A and C could be correct, but which one is the best fit.\n\nThe spokesperson's argument is that because the components are natural, nylon is natural. So that's directly Option A. Option C is more about the substance not being more natural than its least component, which is a bit different. So I think Option A is the correct answer.\n</think>\n\nThe correct answer is:\n\n**A) A substance is natural if the origins of its main components are natural.**\n\n**Explanation:**\nThe nylon industry spokesperson argues that nylon is natural because its main components (petroleum and atmospheric nitrogen) originate from natural sources. This reasoning aligns with Option A, which states that a substance is natural if its main components come from natural origins. The spokesperson's argument directly supports this principle by emphasizing the natural sources of nylon's components.",
