      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I have this logical reasoning problem to solve. Let me read through it carefully and try to understand what's being asked.\n\nThe context given is about antagonism, which is a sensory change phenomenon. It says that antagonism refers to when the presence of one odorous substance weakens the taste characteristics of another odorant. So, in simpler terms, when you have two substances, one can reduce the effect of the other in terms of taste or smell.\n\nThe question is asking which of the options does NOT exhibit antagonism. So, I need to look at each option and determine if it's an example of antagonism or not.\n\nLet me go through each option one by one.\n\nOption A: Adding a small amount of citric acid to orange juice will reduce the sweetness, and adding sugar will reduce the sour taste.\n\nHmm, so in this case, citric acid is added to orange juice. Citric acid is sour, right? So when you add it, it makes the juice more sour. But the statement says it reduces the sweetness. Wait, that's interesting. So adding something sour (citric acid) reduces the sweetness. Similarly, adding sugar reduces the sour taste. So, in both cases, one substance is affecting the perception of another. That sounds like antagonism because each is weakening the other's taste characteristic.\n\nOption B: Saccharin has a bitter taste. Adding a small amount of sodium glutamate to saccharin can significantly alleviate the bitter taste.\n\nSaccharin is known to be sweet, but here it's said to have a bitter taste. Adding sodium glutamate, which is a flavor enhancer, reduces the bitterness. So, the presence of sodium glutamate is weakening the bitter taste of saccharin. That fits the definition of antagonism because one substance (sodium glutamate) is reducing the effect of another (saccharin's bitterness).\n\nOption C: After taking sodium chloride and quinine at the same time, drinking water will have a slightly sweet feeling.\n\nSodium chloride is salt, and quinine is bitter. So, when you take both together and then drink water, the water tastes slightly sweet. That's interesting because water doesn't have any taste, but the combination of salt and quinine is causing a sweet perception. I'm not sure if this is antagonism. Antagonism is about one substance weakening another's taste. Here, it seems like the combination is creating a new taste sensation, perhaps through some interaction, but I'm not certain if it's antagonism. Maybe it's more of a synergistic effect or some kind of taste modification. I'll need to think more about this.\n\nOption D: After eating sour sambal, and then eating sour food, you will not taste the sour taste.\n\nSambal is a spicy condiment, often made with chili peppers, but here it's described as sour. So, after eating sour sambal, when you eat another sour food, you don't taste the sourness. This seems like a case where the first sour taste (sambal) affects the perception of the second sour taste. It's possible that the first sourness reduces the intensity of the second, which would be antagonism. Alternatively, it could be adaptation, where your taste buds get used to the sourness, making the second taste less intense. I'm not entirely sure if this is antagonism or just sensory adaptation.\n\nWait, the definition says it's about the presence of one substance weakening another's taste. So, in D, the first sour substance (sambal) is present when you eat the second sour food, so it's weakening the taste of the second. That would fit the definition of antagonism.\n\nNow, going back to option C. Sodium chloride (salt) and quinine (bitter) are both present, and when you drink water, it tastes sweet. So, the combination of salt and quinine is causing a sweet taste where there wasn't one before. This might be a case of taste interaction, but I'm not sure if it's antagonism. Antagonism is about one substance reducing the effect of another. Here, it's more like the combination creates a new taste. So, perhaps this is not antagonism but a different kind of interaction.\n\nWait, but the question is asking which does NOT exhibit antagonism. So, if C is not an example of antagonism, then it would be the correct answer. Let me think again.\n\nIn C, the presence of both salt and quinine together causes water to taste sweet. So, neither salt nor quinine is directly reducing the other's taste; instead, their combination is altering the perception of water. So, this might not be antagonism. Antagonism is when one substance weakens another's taste. Here, it's more about a combined effect creating a new taste.\n\nSo, putting it all together:\n\nA: Antagonism (citric acid reduces sweetness, sugar reduces sourness)\nB: Antagonism (sodium glutamate reduces bitterness)\nC: Not antagonism (creates a new taste, not weakening another)\nD: Antagonism (sambal reduces the sour taste of the next food)\n\nTherefore, the answer should be option C.\n</think>\n\nThe correct answer is:\n\n**C) After taking sodium chloride and quinine at the same time, drinking water will have a slightly sweet feeling**\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nAntagonism occurs when one substance weakens the taste of another. Options A, B, and D describe scenarios where one substance reduces the effect of another's taste. However, option C involves a combination of substances creating a new taste sensation (sweetness in water) rather than weakening another's taste, thus not exhibiting antagonism.",
